window.CASES.push(
{
  id:"menastream", title:"MENA Stream", type:"AI, Growth strategy", diff:"Medium", mins:30,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["AI","Growth strategy"], sectors:["Media"], stretch:["Structuring","Numeracy"], video:false, rating:4.3, downloads:4206},
  plain:"A Middle East video streaming platform piloted an AI engine that targets ads better. The candidate frames a where-to-play decision (which advertiser segments and markets), reads a bubble chart of pilot results, builds the FMCG business case, and suggests ways to improve the economics before a selective rollout.",
  bottom:"Prioritize <b>Telecom and FMCG</b> (big revenue pools, good uplift, manageable cost-to-serve); Luxury is attractive but small; SMEs are expensive to serve. FMCG case: revenue $21M → ~$28.6M (+$7.6M), minus $2.0M inference and $1.6M support = <b>~$4M net benefit</b>. Recommend <b>scaling selectively, starting with FMCG</b>, while testing premium pricing or minimum spend thresholds.",
  prompt:["Your client, StreamCo, runs an ad-supported video streaming platform across the Middle East.","They have piloted an AI-powered ad targeting engine that improves ad targeting for advertisers on the platform.","They now need to decide whether to scale the AI targeting engine across MENA, which advertiser segments and markets to prioritize, and the optimal rollout model to maximize returns."],
  clarify:[
    ["How does StreamCo make money?","Users pay a low subscription fee in exchange for seeing ads. Advertisers pay StreamCo to show ads, and StreamCo earns more when it sells more ad space and targets ads better."],
    ["What's in scope?","Only whether and how to scale this AI targeting product."],
    ["Could they move to non-targeted ads instead?","No, that's out of scope. The client is committed to targeted ads as the core model."],
    ["How did the pilot go?","We'll look at the pilot results shortly."]
  ],
  openListen:["Plays back the three decisions: whether to scale, where (segments, markets), and how (rollout model)","Asks how StreamCo makes money from ads","Clarifies scope (targeted ads only)"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"3 min",
     say:["Which hypotheses would you test to decide whether and how StreamCo should scale the AI ad-targeting engine across MENA?"],
     steer:["Original case: a generic 'revenues vs. costs' structure is not enough on its own. A strong candidate frames this as a where-to-play decision: which advertiser segments and markets are most attractive."],
     listen:["Revenue upside: price per impression, retention and spend; is the uplift meaningful?","Cost to scale: AI processing, sales and support costs; do benefits exceed costs?","Prioritization: which segments and markets benefit most; is value broad or concentrated?","Execution risks: operations across markets, data protection, low-value segments diluting returns"],
     check:["Revenue: incremental revenue (price per impression, retention, spend)","Revenue: is the uplift meaningful for the economics?","Cost: incremental technology and AI processing costs","Cost: extra sales, support and operating costs","Cost: do benefits outweigh the full cost to scale?","Prioritize: which advertiser segments benefit most","Prioritize: which MENA markets to scale first","Prioritize: broad-based or concentrated value","Risk: operational ability to scale across markets","Risk: data-protection rules varying by market","Risk: low-value segments diluting returns"],
     probe:["\"Would every advertiser benefit equally?\"","\"What could make the rollout fail even if the economics work?\""],
     ifwrong:[["Gives only revenue vs. costs","\"Fine, but where should StreamCo play? Which advertisers and countries first?\""]],
     key:"<p><b>1. Revenue upside</b> (price per impression, advertiser retention and spend; is it meaningful?). <b>2. Cost to scale</b> (AI processing, sales and support; do benefits outweigh costs?). <b>3. Prioritization by segment and market</b> (who benefits most, which markets first, broad or concentrated?). <b>4. Execution risks</b> (operations across markets, data protection, dilution from low-value segments).</p>",
     stuck:["\"Is this decision 'yes or no', or also 'where first'?\""]},
    {name:"Which segments?", time:"4 min",
     say:["The client has segmented its advertiser base into five categories: Telecom, FMCG, Retail, Luxury and SMEs. Here's a chart from the pilot. What stands out, and which segments would you prioritize?"],
     give:{when:"Right away (Exhibit 1).",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 1.</b> Advertiser segments: pilot results (bubble size = current advertiser revenue base)</p><div class="tw"><svg viewBox="0 0 440 270" width="100%" style="max-width:440px;min-width:320px" role="img" aria-label="Bubble chart of segments by AI revenue uplift and cost to serve"><g style="stroke:var(--line)" stroke-width="1"><line x1="50" y1="30" x2="430" y2="30"/><line x1="50" y1="68" x2="430" y2="68"/><line x1="50" y1="106" x2="430" y2="106"/><line x1="50" y1="143" x2="430" y2="143"/><line x1="50" y1="181" x2="430" y2="181"/></g><line x1="50" y1="219" x2="430" y2="219" style="stroke:var(--muted)"/><line x1="50" y1="219" x2="50" y2="30" style="stroke:var(--muted)"/><g style="fill:var(--muted);font-size:11px" text-anchor="end"><text x="44" y="34">25</text><text x="44" y="72">20</text><text x="44" y="110">15</text><text x="44" y="147">10</text><text x="44" y="185">5</text><text x="44" y="223">0</text></g><g style="fill:var(--muted);font-size:11px" text-anchor="middle"><text x="69" y="236">20</text><text x="145" y="236">24</text><text x="221" y="236">28</text><text x="297" y="236">32</text><text x="373" y="236">36</text></g><text x="240" y="258" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--muted);font-size:12px">Revenue uplift from AI (%)</text><text x="50" y="18" style="fill:var(--muted);font-size:12px">Cost-to-serve, % of advertiser revenue</text><g style="fill:var(--accent);opacity:.85"><circle cx="69" cy="113" r="17"><title>Retail: 20% uplift, 14% cost-to-serve</title></circle><circle cx="164" cy="159" r="24"><title>Telecom: 25% uplift, 8% cost-to-serve (largest revenue base)</title></circle><circle cx="221" cy="53" r="11"><title>SMEs: 28% uplift, 22% cost-to-serve</title></circle><circle cx="297" cy="166" r="10"><title>Luxury: 32% uplift, 7% cost-to-serve</title></circle><circle cx="373" cy="143" r="20"><title>FMCG: 36% uplift, 10% cost-to-serve</title></circle></g><g style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:12px"><text x="90" y="117">Retail</text><text x="192" y="152">Telecom</text><text x="236" y="57">SMEs</text><text x="311" y="170">Luxury</text><text x="355" y="115">FMCG</text></g></svg></div><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Segment</th><th>Revenue uplift from AI</th><th>Cost-to-serve (% of revenue)</th><th>Revenue base</th></tr><tr><td>Retail</td><td>~20%</td><td>~14%</td><td>Medium</td></tr><tr><td>Telecom</td><td>~25%</td><td>~8%</td><td>Largest</td></tr><tr><td>SMEs</td><td>~28%</td><td>~22%</td><td>Small</td></tr><tr><td>Luxury</td><td>~32%</td><td>~7%</td><td>Small</td></tr><tr><td>FMCG</td><td>~36%</td><td>~10%</td><td>Large</td></tr></table></div>`},
     listen:["Reads all three dimensions: uplift, cost-to-serve, revenue pool","Telecom and FMCG most attractive: high uplift, big pools, manageable cost","Luxury attractive per account but too small to move the needle","SMEs expensive to serve with a small base, despite decent uplift","Retail in the middle","Value is concentrated, not across the whole base"],
     check:["Telecom and FMCG: high uplift + largest pools + manageable cost","Luxury: strong uplift, low cost, but too small","SMEs: expensive to serve, small base","Retail: some upside, less compelling","Overall: value concentrated in Telecom and FMCG"],
     probe:["\"What does the size of each bubble tell you?\"","\"Why not Luxury, with high uplift and low cost?\""],
     ifwrong:[["Picks Luxury first","\"Look at the size of the bubble. Would it move the needle?\""]],
     key:"<p><b>Telecom and FMCG</b> are most attractive: relatively high uplift, the largest revenue pools, manageable cost-to-serve. <b>Luxury</b> is attractive per account (strong uplift, low cost) but too small. <b>SMEs</b> are expensive to serve with a small base. <b>Retail</b> is in the middle. Most of the value sits in Telecom and FMCG, not across the whole base.</p>",
     stuck:["\"There are three things on this chart. What are they?\""]},
    {name:"FMCG business case", time:"6 min",
     say:["The client wants to know whether the business case works for one of the most promising segments: FMCG. Here are the economics. Is the business case attractive?"],
     steer:["Original case: if the candidate is unsure how to calculate revenue from the data, supply the formulas below."],
     give:{when:"Exhibit right away; the formulas only if they're unsure.",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 2.</b> Economics of scaling the AI engine (annual)</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Metric</th><th>FMCG</th></tr><tr><td>Total ad impressions available</td><td>4.0bn</td></tr><tr><td>Monetized impressions (% of total)</td><td>75%</td></tr><tr><td>Current price per 1,000 impressions</td><td>$7</td></tr><tr><td>AI impact on % monetized</td><td>+10 pp</td></tr><tr><td>AI impact on price</td><td>+20%</td></tr><tr><td>Inference cost per 1,000 impressions processed</td><td>$0.50</td></tr><tr><td>Incremental sales & support cost</td><td>$1.6M</td></tr></table></div><p class="muted">All impressions are AI-processed. If needed: Revenue = monetized impressions ÷ 1,000 × price per 1,000; monetized impressions = total × % monetized.</p>`},
     num:{value:3.96e6, tol:.04, unit:"$ net annual benefit"},
     listen:["States the plan: incremental revenue minus inference and support costs","Current revenue: 4.0bn × 75% ÷ 1,000 × $7 = $21M","Post-AI: 4.0bn × 85% ÷ 1,000 × $8.40 ≈ $28.6M → +$7.6M","Inference: 4.0bn ÷ 1,000 × $0.50 = $2.0M","Net: 7.6 − 2.0 − 1.6 ≈ $4M: attractive","Notes about half the uplift is eaten by costs; pilot results may not hold; support cost creep; privacy rules","Suggests looking at Telecom next"],
     ifwrong:[["Applies the +10pp as +10% of 75%","\"pp means percentage points: 75% becomes 85%.\""],["Applies inference cost only to monetized impressions","\"Which impressions does the AI process?\""]],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">Current: 4.0bn × 75% ÷ 1,000 × $7 = $21.0M\nPost-AI: 4.0bn × 85% ÷ 1,000 × $8.40 ≈ $28.6M\nIncremental revenue ≈ $7.6M\nInference: 4.0bn ÷ 1,000 × $0.50 = $2.0M\nNet benefit ≈ 7.6 − 2.0 − 1.6 = ~$4.0M</p><p>Attractive, but roughly half the uplift goes to inference and support costs, so it only works where pools are large and service is controlled. Risks: pilot uplift may not repeat, support costs creep, privacy rules differ by market. Analyze Telecom next.</p>",
     stuck:["\"How do you turn impressions into revenue?\"","\"Which numbers change with AI?\""]},
    {name:"Improving the economics", time:"3–4 min",
     say:["The client is encouraged, but wants to improve returns before scaling more broadly. They don't want to restrict access too aggressively, because the AI engine is becoming an important part of the sales story. How could StreamCo improve the economics while keeping commercial momentum?"],
     listen:["Capture more value per advertiser","Reduce cost to serve","Strengthen the AI proposition to hold the price premium"],
     check:["Charge a higher premium for AI targeting","Minimum spend thresholds so AI is used where it pays back","Performance-based pricing","Bundle AI into premium advertiser tiers","Sales incentives toward high-value AI accounts","Self-serve tools for smaller accounts","Less manual support for lower-value advertisers","Standardized onboarding and campaign setup","Data partnerships (telcos, payment platforms)","New AI ad formats (shoppable, interactive)"],
     probe:["\"How could you serve small advertisers more cheaply?\"","\"How do you keep advertisers paying a premium?\""],
     key:"<p><b>Capture more value</b>: higher premium, minimum spend thresholds, performance-based pricing, premium bundles, sales incentives. <b>Reduce cost to serve</b>: self-serve tools, less manual support, standardized onboarding. <b>Strengthen the proposition</b>: data partnerships, shoppable and interactive AI ad formats.</p>"},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["What is your overall recommendation at this stage?"],
     listen:["Answer first: scale selectively, starting with FMCG, not a universal rollout","Value is real but concentrated","FMCG case positive (~$4M) after all costs, but not big enough to scale everywhere automatically","Improve pricing and support economics before expanding","Next: FMCG phase, test premium pricing or minimum spend, validate in largest markets","Risk: too fast dilutes returns; too slow underinvests"],
     key:"<p>\"You asked whether and how to scale the AI ad-targeting engine. I'd scale selectively, starting with FMCG, rather than universally, while improving pricing and support economics. The upside is real but concentrated: FMCG combines a large pool, strong uplift and manageable cost, and its case is positive at about $4M a year after inference and support costs. Next, launch with FMCG, test premium pricing or minimum spend thresholds, and validate in the largest markets. The risk: too broad too fast dilutes returns; too cautious underinvests in a capability that's working.\"</p>"}
  ]
},
{
  id:"newlight", title:"Newlight Solar Cells", type:"Product launch", diff:"Medium", mins:30,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["Product launch"], sectors:["Energy"], stretch:["Numeracy","Judgement & insights"], video:false, rating:4.4, downloads:7169},
  plain:"A European solar company wants to launch thin-film solar cells (built into windows) in Germany. The candidate sizes the household market, adjusts it for sunlight and shading, chooses a go-to-market route (B2B vs. B2C, which segment), and sizes the renovation market as a first beachhead.",
  bottom:"Households: ~40M × ~7.6 windows ≈ 304M m² × €200 ≈ <b>€61B</b> theoretical. Adjust: −50% for north/east/west windows, −20% for shading → <b>~€24B</b>. Go <b>B2B through renovation companies</b> (fast, cheaper, open to innovation). Windows last ~20 years, so renovation market ≈ <b>€1.2B a year</b>.",
  prompt:["Our client is Newlight, a European high-tech solar power equipment producer that wants to enter the German market with its latest innovation: 'thin-film' solar cells.","Thin-film cells are placed within glass windows, unlike conventional standalone solar cells. They're generally cheaper, but less efficient at converting energy.","The relevant market is in principle every property with windows in Germany. However, the film only works with smooth window surfaces, and windows must be fully replaced rather than retrofitted.","Newlight has no sales structure or business in Germany yet, but it's the first mover, with no competition expected soon. The CEO wants to know: what's the overall market potential in Germany, and what market entry strategy should Newlight use?"],
  clarify:[
    ["Is there any competition?","None expected in the near future: Newlight is the first mover."],
    ["Does Newlight have a presence in Germany?","No sales structure or business set up there yet."],
    ["Can existing windows be retrofitted?","No, windows must be completely replaced."],
    ["What's the price?","We'll come to pricing shortly."]
  ],
  openListen:["Plays back both questions: market potential AND entry strategy","Notes the constraints: full window replacement, no German presence","Notes first-mover advantage"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"3 min",
     say:["How would you structure your approach to the question?"],
     listen:["Market size: quantity (buildings by type × windows × penetration) and price (payoff, production cost, margin)","Entry options: B2C vs. B2B (construction, renovation, window makers)","Capabilities: local relationships, sales capabilities, regulation"],
     check:["Size: number of houses, public and commercial buildings","Size: windows per building type","Size: penetration rate by building type","Price: electricity generated per window (payoff)","Price: production cost and margin","Entry: B2C (website, TV ads, door-to-door)","Entry: B2B: construction industry","Entry: B2B: renovation companies","Entry: B2B: window manufacturers","Capability: local relationships in property/construction","Capability: local sales skills (language)","Capability: German regulations"],
     probe:["\"How would you actually count the windows in Germany?\"","\"Who could sell this product for Newlight?\""],
     key:"<p><b>1. Market size</b>: quantity (buildings by type, windows per building, penetration) and price (payoff per window, production cost and margin). <b>2. Entry options</b>: B2C (website, TV, door-to-door) vs. B2B (construction, renovation, window manufacturers). <b>3. Capabilities</b>: local relationships, sales skills, regulation.</p>"},
    {name:"Household market", time:"5–6 min",
     say:["The client aims to price a 1m² thin-film panel at €200, which also reflects the value of the electricity it could generate in a year. Focusing only on private households in Germany, what's the theoretical market size?"],
     steer:["'Theoretical' means if every available window were fitted. Let the candidate estimate; offer the hints below if needed."],
     give:{when:"If required or helpful.",
       html:"<ul><li>Assume every window is the same size: <b>1m²</b></li><li>Average household: <b>2 people</b></li><li>Split homes into 3 sizes; one window per room, plus <b>4 extra windows</b> for kitchen and bathroom</li><li>We want the theoretical maximum: all available windows fitted</li></ul>"},
     num:{min:40e9, max:90e9, unit:"€ theoretical market"},
     listen:["~80M people ÷ 2 = ~40M households","Segments homes: small (6 windows), medium (8), large (12)","96M + 160M + 48M = 304M m²","× €200 = ~€61B","Notes it assumes 100% adoption, but excludes commercial and public buildings"],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">80M people ÷ 2 = 40M households\nSmall 40%: 16M × 6 = 96M m²\nMedium 50%: 20M × 8 = 160M m²\nLarge 10%: 4M × 12 = 48M m²\nTotal 304M m² × €200 ≈ €60.8B</p><p>Very large, but assumes every household adopts; it also excludes commercial and public buildings.</p>",
     stuck:["\"How many households are there in Germany?\"","\"How many windows does a typical home have?\""]},
    {name:"Sun and shading", time:"3–4 min",
     say:["Because of the sun's path during the day and dense housing in cities, some adjustments are needed. What's a reasonable reduction in the market size, given natural physical limits on the relevant windows?"],
     give:{when:"If required.", html:"<p>The sun's radiation is strongest on <b>south-facing</b> windows, and there's none on <b>north-facing</b> windows.</p>"},
     num:{min:12e9, max:40e9, unit:"€ adjusted market"},
     listen:["North 0%, East/West 50%, South 100% → average 50%","Shading in dense cities: a further reduction, e.g. 20%","€60.8B × 0.5 × 0.8 ≈ €24.3B","Still very large, before commercial and public buildings"],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">Orientation: (0% + 50% + 50% + 100%) ÷ 4 = 50% → €30.4B\nShading (urban Germany): −20% → €24.3B</p><p>Less than half the original, but still very large.</p>",
     stuck:["\"Which way do windows face, and how much sun does each get?\""]},
    {name:"Entry strategy", time:"4 min",
     say:["Newlight has no sales structure in Germany yet, so it's considering all options. Which sales strategies suit an efficient, rapid development of the German market? And which segments of the real estate market look most promising?"],
     steer:["Original case: if the candidate is unsure, ask them to think creatively about segments and strategies first, then judge which suits this client and product.","Many B2B answers are acceptable. If they don't land on renovation companies, say afterwards that those are likely the most attractive: they have incentives to innovate and add value for clients."],
     listen:["Compares direct B2C vs. indirect B2B","B2C: time- and cost-intensive, needs mass education","B2B: one partner gives access to many windows; fast growth, economies of scale","Lists B2B segments: construction, window and glass makers, architects, developers, renovation","Picks renovation firms: no opportunity cost, open to innovation"],
     check:["B2C via website, TV ads, door-to-door: slow and costly","B2B partnerships: fast access to many properties, scale","Segments: construction companies","Segments: window / glass manufacturers","Segments: architects, property developers","Segments: renovation and maintenance firms (most promising)"],
     probe:["\"Why would one partnership be worth more than many direct sales?\"","\"Which type of company is already replacing windows?\""],
     key:"<p><b>B2C</b> (web, TV, door-to-door) is the most time- and cost-intensive and needs mass education. <b>B2B</b> partnerships give access to many properties per deal, so faster growth and scale. The best segment is likely <b>renovation companies</b>: no opportunity cost, since windows are being replaced anyway, and an incentive to offer innovative value to their clients.</p>"},
    {name:"Renovation market", time:"3 min",
     say:["As a first step, the client will sell only to firms renovating private houses. What's the annual size of this market?"],
     give:{when:"If required.", html:"<p>The average lifetime of a window is <b>20 years</b>. We still want the theoretical market size.</p>"},
     num:{min:0.6e9, max:2.3e9, unit:"€ per year"},
     listen:["Windows replaced every ~20 years, evenly spread → 1/20 of homes a year","€24.3B ÷ 20 ≈ €1.2B a year","Calls it a large, attractive beachhead"],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">€24.32B × 1/20 ≈ €1.2B a year</p>",
     stuck:["\"How often does a home replace its windows?\""]},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["Based on what we've covered, what's your overall recommendation to the client?"],
     listen:["Answer first: enter Germany via partnerships with renovation companies","Reasons: B2B is faster and cheaper than B2C; renovation firms most open to innovation; ~€1.2B a year in renovations alone","Next steps: target major renovation players; later assess commercial and public buildings"],
     key:"<p>\"We were asked how big the opportunity is and how to enter. It looks very attractive, and Newlight should enter by partnering with renovation companies: B2B is faster and cheaper than B2C; renovation firms are the most open to innovative products; and the renovation market alone is about €1.2B a year. Next, identify the major renovation players and build a business-development plan; then assess commercial and public buildings.\"</p>"}
  ]
},
{
  id:"cbafashion", title:"CBA Fashion", type:"Operations", diff:"Medium", mins:30,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["Operations"], sectors:["Retail"], stretch:["Structuring","Judgement & insights"], video:true, rating:4.4, downloads:8988},
  plain:"A small UK online menswear retailer wants to cut shipping time from 3.5 to 2 days. The candidate maps the order process (inbound, warehouse, outbound), finds the slow French supplier delays ~20% of orders, switches outbound shipping to a faster tracked carrier within budget, and manages the quality risk.",
  bottom:"Process: inbound 1 day + warehouse 0.5 + outbound 2 = 3.5 days. <b>Swap the French supplier</b>: it delays ~19–20% of orders (orders wait for their slowest item) and takes <b>3 days</b>; replacing it saves <b>0.5 day</b>. <b>Switch to Apollo's tracked 1-day delivery</b> (+£0.50, within the £0.80 budget): saves <b>1 day</b>. Total 1.5 days → 2 days, with a trial and parallel run to protect quality.",
  prompt:["Our client is CBA Fashion, a small but growing fashion e-commerce company selling menswear in the UK. It uses bespoke algorithms to recommend clothing and outfits to men, and ships clothes across the country.","The client doesn't hold its own stock: when a customer orders, it orders from its suppliers, consolidates the items in its own warehouse, then ships them to the customer.","Partly because of this model, shipping is slow: on average 3.5 days from order to delivery. The client wants to cut 1.5 days, to an average of 2 days.","How can CBA Fashion reduce shipping time to an average of 2 days?"],
  clarify:[
    ["What exactly is 'shipping time'?","The time from when a customer places an order to when it arrives at their address."],
    ["What are the steps in the 3.5 days?","Three main steps: inbound logistics (suppliers to our warehouse) 1 day; warehouse processing 0.5 days; outbound logistics (warehouse to customers) 2 days."],
    ["Any cost constraints?","The client wants to avoid big investments now, and can only increase overall processing cost by up to £0.80 per order."]
  ],
  openListen:["Confirms the definition of shipping time","Asks for the process breakdown (1 + 0.5 + 2 days)","Asks about cost constraints (£0.80)"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"3 min",
     say:["How would you structure your approach to the problem?"],
     steer:["Original case: this case tests prioritization: the order of topics can follow the candidate's choices.","Three topics yield no time savings. Discuss briefly, but hint strongly they're not areas of interest: warehouse processing (already optimized; carriers won't collect more often without 5× volume), drop-shipping (client avoids it: loses control of experience, multiple parcels), holding inventory (not practical at this scale; needs wide SKU variety for recommendations)."],
     listen:["Optimize the current process: inbound, warehouse, outbound","Redesign the process: drop-ship, hold inventory","Uses the step times to prioritize (outbound 2 days is biggest)"],
     check:["Inbound: speed up supplier logistics","Inbound: run our own logistics","Warehouse: faster processing","Outbound: faster with current carrier","Outbound: faster with new carriers","Redesign: drop-ship from suppliers to customers","Redesign: hold inventory"],
     probe:["\"Where does most of the 3.5 days go?\"","\"Could the process itself look different?\""],
     ifwrong:[["Wants to dig into warehouse processing","\"It's already as optimized as possible at this scale. Where else could we look?\""]],
     key:"<p><b>1. Optimize the current process</b>: inbound (speed up supplier logistics, run our own), warehouse speed, outbound (current or new carriers). <b>2. Redesign the process</b>: drop-ship from suppliers, hold inventory.</p>"},
    {name:"Inbound options", time:"3 min",
     say:["One option is to shorten inbound logistics: the time for items to reach us from suppliers. This step takes 1 day on average. What opportunities exist to reduce it?"],
     steer:["Confirm inbound takes 1 day on average. Share the facts below only if asked. Before saying how much slower the French supplier is, move on to the next question."],
     give:{when:"If asked.",
       html:"<ul><li>10 suppliers, with roughly equal volume</li><li>If we drop a supplier, we can find similar brands to replace it</li><li>One supplier is in France; the rest are in the UK</li><li>The French supplier is notably slower to ship</li><li>Suppliers can't be sped up; we've already pressured them</li><li>We lack the scale to run our own inbound logistics, and it wouldn't help much: most of the time is suppliers' internal processing</li></ul>"},
     listen:["Asks about the supplier mix","Spots variance between suppliers","Can't speed suppliers or run own logistics → rationalize the supplier mix","Swap the French supplier for a local one, matching style and price"],
     key:"<p>Inbound time varies by supplier. Since we can't run our own inbound logistics or speed suppliers up, <b>rationalize the supplier mix</b>: swap the French supplier for a local alternative, matching style and pricing to avoid a gap in the range.</p>",
     stuck:["\"Are all suppliers equally fast?\""]},
    {name:"French supplier math", time:"5 min",
     say:["Switching out the supplier looks promising, but let's confirm the time saved. The French supplier is 10% of items shipped; orders contain 2 items on average; all UK suppliers ship to us in 0.5 days. How long does the French supplier take? And what's the overall impact of swapping it out?"],
     steer:["Original case: the key insight is that orders move at the speed of their slowest item.","If they ask or get stuck, say explicitly: orders wait in our warehouse until complete, so the 10% of items from France slow down more than 10% of orders."],
     num:{value:3, tol:.05, unit:"days for the French supplier"},
     listen:["Orders wait for their slowest item","P(at least one French item) = 10% + 10% − 1% = 19% ≈ 20%","1 = 0.8 × 0.5 + 0.2 × X → X = 3 days","Swapping it brings inbound to 0.5 days: saves half a day overall"],
     ifwrong:[["Uses 10% of orders","\"An order has two items. If either is French, what happens to the order?\""]],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">P(order has a French item) = 10% + 10% − 10%×10% = 19% ≈ 20%\n1 day = 0.8 × 0.5 + 0.2 × X → 0.6 = 0.2X → X = 3 days\nSwap → inbound 0.5 days → saves 0.5 day overall</p>",
     stuck:["\"When can an order leave the warehouse?\"","\"What share of orders contain at least one French item?\""]},
    {name:"Outbound options", time:"3 min",
     say:["Another option is to shorten outbound logistics: from our warehouse to the customer. This takes 2 days on average. What opportunities exist to reduce it?"],
     steer:["Confirm outbound takes 2 days. Share the facts below only if asked."],
     give:{when:"If asked.",
       html:"<ul><li>We use the national mail service's Tracked 48 (a 2-day service)</li><li>Their 1-day option would either cost <b>£1.20 more per order</b> (too high) or be <b>untracked</b> (not acceptable for quality)</li><li>We lack the scale to run our own outbound logistics</li><li>A new carrier, <b>Apollo Deliveries</b>, offers tracked 1-day delivery for <b>£0.50</b> more than today</li><li>We can onboard them, but must watch quality</li></ul>"},
     listen:["Asks about current carrier and options","Rules out £1.20 (over the £0.80 budget) and untracked service","Switch to Apollo: tracked, 1-day, +£0.50 within budget → saves 1 day","Flags quality risk"],
     key:"<p>With tracking required and a £0.80 budget, the best option is to <b>switch to Apollo Deliveries</b>: tracked 1-day delivery for +£0.50, saving <b>1 day</b> on every order. Watch for quality issues.</p>",
     stuck:["\"What are the options for faster delivery, and what do they cost?\""]},
    {name:"Managing quality risk", time:"3 min",
     say:["We want to be sure changing carrier won't cause quality issues such as late deliveries or lost parcels. How would you mitigate that risk?"],
     steer:["After they answer, share that following these checks, the client is comfortable enough to switch."],
     listen:["Two angles: lower the likelihood, and reduce the impact","Likelihood: research, references, trial run","Impact: run both carriers in parallel, compensation clauses"],
     check:["Desktop research on the supplier (reviews)","Ask others in the industry","Trial run to test real performance","Run both carriers in parallel for a while","Compensation clause for quality issues"],
     key:"<p><b>Lower the likelihood</b>: desktop research and reviews, industry references, a trial run. <b>Reduce the impact</b>: run both carriers in parallel so we can switch back, and a compensation clause to incentivize performance.</p>"},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["Overall, what would you recommend to the client?"],
     listen:["Answer first: two changes deliver the 1.5 days","Swap the French supplier: −0.5 day","Switch to Apollo's tracked next-day delivery: −1 day, within budget","Next: implement while protecting quality (trial, parallel run)"],
     key:"<p>\"We were asked how to cut shipping time by 1.5 days. Two steps get there. First, replace the slow French supplier with a local one, saving half a day, because orders wait for their slowest item. Second, switch outbound to Apollo's tracked next-day service, saving a full day for £0.50 more per order, within budget. Over the coming weeks, implement both while protecting quality with a trial and a parallel run.\"</p>"}
  ]
},
{
  id:"qtco", title:"QT Co.", type:"Profitability", diff:"Easy", mins:20,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["Profitability"], sectors:["Consumer Goods"], stretch:[], video:true, rating:4.5, downloads:11677},
  plain:"A US baby-products leader (strollers, car seats) has seen profit fall 30% in two years to $70M and wants $100M. The candidate structures profitability, reads two charts (falling volumes; sales shifting online), sizes the profit from selling online, and gives a 30-second update.",
  bottom:"Volume fell <b>25%</b> (1.2M → ~900k units), explaining the profit drop. Online is rising (~7% of the market, ~20% in 3 years) but explains only part of it. Selling online (+10% volume) adds <b>~$7M</b> profit: still <b>$23M short</b> of $100M. Recommend going online and investigating the wider volume decline.",
  prompt:["Your client is QT Co., a US baby-products manufacturer and the market leader in strollers and car seats. It offers the safest products on the market, which parents value greatly, at prices competitive with other premium brands.","Despite its leadership, profits have dwindled over the past two years: last quarter was 30% below the same quarter two years earlier.","The CEO wants to correct this trend. What can QT Co. do to increase profitability?"],
  clarify:[
    ["What's the goal?","$100 million of annual profit, as soon as possible."],
    ["Where are profits now?","$70 million, down 30% from two years ago."],
    ["Where do they sell?","Only the US and Canada."],
    ["What products?","Three: Strollerall, Care seat and Duo. All have seen falling volume."],
    ["What about competitors?","QT Co.'s main rival has started selling exclusively online."],
    ["How's the market doing?","The overall market has been growing."],
    ["How do they sell?","Through big-box and specialty retailers."]
  ],
  openListen:["Asks for the target ($100M) and current profit ($70M)","Asks about products, channels and competitors","Notices: the market grows while QT shrinks"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"2–3 min",
     say:["How can QT Co. improve its profitability?"],
     listen:["Increase revenues: volume and price, by product, channel and geography","Lower costs: fixed (manufacturing overhead, marketing, R&D) and variable (unit cost, warranties, distribution)"],
     check:["Revenue by products","Revenue by sales channels","Revenue by geography","Fixed: manufacturing overhead","Fixed: marketing","Fixed: R&D","Variable: manufacturing unit cost","Variable: warranties","Variable: distribution"],
     probe:["\"Which angles would you cut revenue by?\"","\"What costs does a stroller maker have?\""],
     key:"<p><b>1. Increase revenues</b> (volume and price) by product, by sales channel, by geography. <b>2. Lower costs</b>: fixed (manufacturing overhead, marketing, R&D) and variable (unit manufacturing cost, warranties, distribution).</p>"},
    {name:"Reading the exhibits", time:"4 min",
     say:["We've gathered information on QT Co.'s sales volumes by product and the market's sales by channel. What can you conclude from these exhibits?"],
     give:{when:"Right away (Exhibits 1 and 2).",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 1.</b> QT Co. sales volume by product (units a year)</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Product</th><th>Y-2</th><th>Y-1</th><th>Y0</th></tr><tr><td>Strollerall</td><td>400k</td><td>~370k</td><td>~350k</td></tr><tr><td>Care seat</td><td>400k</td><td>~350k</td><td>~300k</td></tr><tr><td>Duo</td><td>400k</td><td>~300k</td><td>~225k</td></tr><tr><td><b>Total</b></td><td><b>1.2M</b></td><td><b>~1.02M</b></td><td><b>~0.9M</b></td></tr></table></div><p><b>Exhibit 2.</b> Market sales value by channel (share of total)</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Year</th><th>Y-6</th><th>Y-4</th><th>Y-2</th><th>Y-1</th><th>Y0</th><th>Y1E</th><th>Y2E</th><th>Y3E</th></tr><tr><td>Online</td><td>1%</td><td>2%</td><td>4%</td><td>5%</td><td>7%</td><td>10%</td><td>15%</td><td>20%</td></tr><tr><td>Specialty</td><td>25%</td><td>24%</td><td>23%</td><td>23%</td><td>22%</td><td>22%</td><td>21%</td><td>21%</td></tr><tr><td>Big box</td><td>74%</td><td>74%</td><td>73%</td><td>72%</td><td>71%</td><td>68%</td><td>64%</td><td>59%</td></tr></table></div>`},
     listen:["Total volume fell ~25% (1.2M → ~900k): likely explains the 30% profit decline","Every product fell; Duo fell most","Online gaining share (~7% now, ~20% in 3 years), at big-box and specialty's expense","Online explains only a small part of the volume decline","Next: should QT sell online? What else caused the decline?"],
     probe:["\"Which product fell fastest?\"","\"Can the online shift alone explain a 25% drop?\""],
     key:"<p>Overall volume fell <b>25% in two years</b> (1.2M → ~900k), which likely explains the 30% profit drop. <b>Online is gaining</b>, ~7% of the market now and expected ~20% in 3 years, at the expense of big-box and specialty, but that explains only a small part of our decline. Next: should we sell online, and what else is driving the volume loss?</p>"},
    {name:"Profit from online", time:"5 min",
     say:["The client understands the issue isn't product quality but the channel. An internal analysis shows selling online could boost volumes by 10% per item. How much would this increase QT Co.'s profits?"],
     give:{when:"If asked.", html:"<ul><li>Prices: Strollerall <b>$400</b>; Care seat <b>$250</b>; Duo <b>$600</b></li><li>Profit margin: <b>20%</b> on each product</li><li>Current volumes (Y0): 350k, 300k, 225k</li></ul>"},
     num:{value:7e6, tol:.03, unit:"$ extra profit a year"},
     listen:["Profit increase = extra volume × profit per unit","Strollerall: 35k × $80 = $2.8M","Care seat: 30k × $50 = $1.5M","Duo: 22.5k × $120 = $2.7M","Total $7M: +10%, but $23M short of $100M","Caveats: setup cost, cannibalization, conflict with retailers"],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">Strollerall: 35k × $80 = $2.8M\nCare seat: 30k × $50 = $1.5M\nDuo: 22.5k × $120 = $2.7M\nTotal ≈ $7M (70 → 77, still $23M short of $100M)</p><p>Online setup is costly, may cannibalize revenue and upset offline distributors. Next: which items, what price, fulfillment; and other growth (new product line, international).</p>",
     stuck:["\"What's the profit on one Strollerall?\""]},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1 min",
     say:["The CEO just walked into the team room and wants a quick, 30-second synthesis of what you've found. What do you tell her?"],
     listen:["Restates the goal ($100M)","Recommend selling online: market shifting to ~20% online in 3 years; +$7M a year","Only ~25% of the gap: also investigate why volume fell while the market grew (range, pricing, channel performance)"],
     key:"<p>\"You asked how QT Co. can get back to $100M of profit. We recommend selling online: the market is moving there, to about 20% in three years, and it would add about $7M a year. That's only about a quarter of the gap, so while we build the online channel, we need to find out why our volume fell despite a growing market, looking at our range, pricing and performance in existing channels.\"</p>"}
  ]
}
);
