window.CASES.push(
{
  id:"quantumbank", title:"Quantum Bank", type:"AI, Cost reduction", diff:"Medium", mins:30,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["AI"], sectors:["Financial Services"], stretch:["Judgement & insights"], video:false, rating:null, downloads:null},
  plain:"A large European bank wants to use AI to automate work in its retail banking division and cut costs. The candidate structures how to assess AI use cases, brainstorms where AI fits across the bank, suggests 'moonshot' ideas, then allocates a €10M budget across five projects, some of which only pay off if fully funded.",
  bottom:"Fund <b>Loan Application Review (€5M → €10M)</b> and <b>Fraud Monitoring AI (€4M → €7M)</b> in full, then put the last <b>€1M into the Customer Service Chatbot</b> (linear: €1.67M). Total <b>~€18.7M a year</b> of savings, nearly 2× the budget. Compliance Automation has a high ROI but would block the better combination.",
  prompt:["Your client is Quantum Bank, one of Europe's largest banks, with Retail Banking, Corporate Banking, Wealth Management and Capital Markets.","Facing cost pressure, regulatory demands and rising customer expectations, its leaders are evaluating AI-powered automation to optimize internal operations and reduce costs.","Some peer banks have started targeted AI projects; Quantum Bank is considering a large-scale AI automation program, focused on its Retail Banking division.","The Retail Banking COO needs your support to assess the opportunity."],
  clarify:[
    ["How big is Retail Banking?","Over 15,000 employees."],
    ["How much of the cost base is it?","Around 40% of the bank's total operating costs."],
    ["Is there a target or budget?","No specific targets yet. We can discuss the budget later in the case."],
    ["Is the goal cost or revenue?","Mainly cost reduction, though revenue upside counts too."]
  ],
  openListen:["Plays back: assess a large-scale AI automation program in Retail Banking","Asks about scale (employees, share of costs)","Asks about targets and budget"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"3 min",
     say:["How would you structure your overall assessment of the AI automation opportunity to determine how Quantum Bank should proceed?"],
     listen:["Use case identification: which processes suit AI","Financial impact: savings, revenue uplift, investment and payback","Capabilities and readiness: data and organization"],
     check:["Use cases: process repetition and volume","Use cases: complexity of rules (clear rules vs. expert judgment)","Use cases: impact of automation failure (financial, reputational, regulatory)","Use cases: existing industry use cases and benchmarks","Financial: cost savings (labor)","Financial: error-reduction savings (rework, penalties)","Financial: revenue uplift (faster approvals, personalization)","Financial: investment and payback (build, integration, run)","Data: availability, quality, governance","Organization: leadership buy-in","Organization: AI talent","Organization: change management (adoption, unions, retraining)","Organization: AI governance (explainability, auditability, regulation)"],
     probe:["\"How would you decide which processes to automate first?\"","\"What could stop the bank from capturing the savings?\""],
     key:"<p><b>1. Use case identification</b>: repetition and volume, complexity of rules, impact of failure, industry benchmarks. <b>2. Financial impact</b>: cost savings (labor, fewer errors), revenue uplift (faster credit, personalization), investment and payback. <b>3. Capabilities and readiness</b>: data (availability, quality, governance) and organization (leadership buy-in, AI talent, change management, AI governance).</p>"},
    {name:"Where AI fits", time:"4 min",
     say:["Before diving into financial analysis, the COO wants to make sure all relevant areas are considered. Please outline examples of key processes across the bank's operations that may benefit from AI solutions."],
     steer:["Original case: the candidate should structure the opportunities by the bank's operational areas. If they start listing use cases at random, encourage them to go area by area.","If they gave prioritization criteria in their structure (e.g. repetitiveness), they should use them here. If not, ask how they'd assess these use cases.","If they don't know banking and focus on one or two areas, tell them the other key areas (below) and ask how AI could help each."],
     give:{when:"Only if they are unfamiliar with banking.", html:"<p>Key areas: <b>customer service</b>, <b>credit and loan operations</b>, <b>fraud and risk monitoring</b>, <b>regulatory compliance</b>, <b>internal back-office efficiency</b>.</p>"},
     listen:["Goes area by area, not a random list","Uses criteria: volume, rule-based, risk if wrong","Customer service: high volume, low risk → strong","Credit/loans: structured data, clear rules; some moderate risk","Fraud: real-time pattern spotting; needs explainability","Compliance: rule-heavy but higher regulatory risk","Back office: repetitive, low risk, ideal for savings"],
     check:["Service: chatbots for common questions","Service: voice and sentiment routing","Service: email and message triage","Credit: document recognition (income, ID)","Credit: AI credit scoring incl. alternative data","Credit: instant pre-approvals","Fraud: real-time anomaly detection","Fraud: predictive fraud flags","Fraud: dynamic customer risk profiles","Compliance: KYC/AML checks and onboarding","Compliance: watchlist and embargo screening","Compliance: standard regulatory reports","Back office: staff scheduling","Back office: predictive maintenance (ATMs, IT)","Back office: process mining and automation"],
     probe:["\"Which of these would you do first, using your criteria?\""],
     key:"<p><b>Customer service</b> (high volume, low risk): chatbots, voice and sentiment routing, message triage. <b>Credit and loans</b> (structured data, clear rules, some risk): document checks, AI credit scoring, instant pre-approvals. <b>Fraud and risk</b> (real-time patterns, high cost of misses, explainability needed): anomaly detection, predictive flags, risk profiles. <b>Compliance</b> (rule-heavy, high scrutiny): KYC/AML, watchlist screening, regulatory reports. <b>Back office</b> (repetitive, low risk): scheduling, predictive maintenance, process mining.</p>"},
    {name:"Moonshot ideas", time:"3 min",
     say:["Now imagine Quantum Bank wants to position itself as an AI innovation leader in banking: not just efficient, but cutting-edge. What 'moonshot' or unconventional AI use case could it explore to stand out?"],
     steer:["Original case: the idea doesn't need to be feasible right away. Push for 2–3 ideas much more ambitious than the previous answer."],
     listen:["2–3 ideas clearly beyond automation","Each with a clear value to customers or the bank"],
     check:["Personal AI banker / customer digital twin","Real-time AI financial coaching for SMEs","Predictive churn prevention","Emotion-based security (detect a customer under threat)","AI life-stage concierge (baby, home purchase)","Synthetic customer personas to test products and policies"],
     probe:["\"Push further: what would make a customer switch banks for this?\""],
     key:"<p>Examples: a <b>personal AI banker</b> (a digital twin that advises, simulates and negotiates rates); <b>AI coaching for SMEs</b> on cash flow and tax; <b>predictive churn prevention</b>; <b>emotion-based security</b> to detect customers under threat; an <b>AI life-stage concierge</b>; <b>synthetic personas</b> to test products and lending policies safely.</p>"},
    {name:"Budget allocation", time:"6 min",
     say:["Quantum Bank has approved a €10 million budget for AI in Retail Banking. The COO has shortlisted five use cases with different costs, savings and investment profiles. Based on Exhibit 1, how should Quantum allocate the €10 million to maximize total annual cost savings?"],
     give:{when:"Right away (Exhibit 1).",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 1.</b> Selected AI use cases: cost, impact and investment profile</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Use case</th><th>Implementation cost (€M)</th><th>Potential annual cost reduction (€M)</th><th>Investment profile</th></tr><tr><td>Customer Service Chatbot</td><td>3</td><td>5</td><td>Linear</td></tr><tr><td>Loan Application Review</td><td>5</td><td>10</td><td>All-or-nothing</td></tr><tr><td>Compliance Automation</td><td>2</td><td>3.5</td><td>All-or-nothing</td></tr><tr><td>Fraud Monitoring AI</td><td>4</td><td>7</td><td>All-or-nothing</td></tr><tr><td>Call Center Forecasting</td><td>4</td><td>4.5</td><td>Linear</td></tr></table></div><p class="muted">Linear projects save in proportion to what's invested. All-or-nothing projects save only once fully funded.</p>`},
     num:{value:18.67e6, tol:.02, unit:"€ annual savings"},
     listen:["Computes ROI per use case (savings ÷ cost)","Loan 2.0; Compliance 1.75; Fraud 1.75; Chatbot 1.67; Forecasting 1.13","Funds Loan (€5M), then Fraud (€4M) rather than Compliance","Puts the last €1M into the Chatbot (linear) → €1.67M","Total ≈ €18.7M a year, ~1.9× ROI","Insight: ranking by ROI alone fails with all-or-nothing projects"],
     ifwrong:[["Funds Compliance after Loan (ROI tie)","\"Check what's left after that. Can you then fund the rest well?\" (Loan + Compliance + Chatbot = €18.5M < €18.67M)"],["Puts €1M into Compliance or Fraud","\"Those are all-or-nothing. What does €1M of Compliance save?\""]],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">ROI: Loan 10÷5 = 2.00 · Compliance 3.5÷2 = 1.75 · Fraud 7÷4 = 1.75 · Chatbot 5÷3 = 1.67 · Forecasting 4.5÷4 = 1.13\nLoan Review (€5M) → €10M · left €5M\nFraud Monitoring (€4M) → €7M · left €1M\nChatbot €1M × (5÷3) → €1.67M\nTotal ≈ €18.67M a year on €10M</p><p>Picking Compliance instead of Fraud gives Loan + Compliance + €3M Chatbot = €18.5M. Two projects deliver ~91% of savings. Prioritizing by ROI alone misleads when projects are all-or-nothing.</p>",
     stuck:["\"What's the return per euro on each project?\"","\"What can you do with leftover money that won't fully fund a project?\""]},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["You're meeting the Retail Banking COO. He knows this is preliminary, but wants your initial recommendation. What would you tell him?"],
     listen:["Answer first: deploy the full €10M into three initiatives","~€18.7M a year of recurring savings, nearly 2× ROI","Impact concentrated: Loan + Fraud ≈ 91%","All-or-nothing projects change the logic","Execution: Loan Review is sensitive, so needs governance and regulator dialogue","Next: owners and targets, central AI team, reassess more use cases, explore moonshots"],
     key:"<p>\"You asked us to assess AI automation in Retail Banking. Deploy the full €10M into three initiatives: Loan Application Review, Fraud Monitoring AI, and a partly funded Customer Service Chatbot. That's about €18.7M of annual savings, nearly 2× the investment. Two initiatives deliver about 91% of it and can start now. Some projects only pay off if fully funded, so ranking by ROI alone would mislead. Loan Review is sensitive, so pair it with strong governance and regulator dialogue. Next: approve the allocation, assign owners and targets, set up a central AI delivery and governance team, reassess other use cases after the first wave, and start exploring the moonshots.\"</p>"}
  ]
},
{
  id:"mcburger", title:"McBurger Co", type:"Growth strategy", diff:"Hard", mins:35,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["Growth strategy"], sectors:["Retail"], stretch:["Numeracy","Judgement & insights"], video:false, rating:4.5, downloads:13284},
  plain:"A global fast-food chain wants to grow faster in China. The candidate reads an income-distribution chart by city tier (the middle class is exploding), calculates how much revenue would rise if customers were as engaged as competitors' customers, solves an algebra problem for a bigger target, and brainstorms engagement ideas.",
  bottom:"The Mainstream segment will roughly <b>triple in Tier 1</b> and become the majority in <b>Tier 2–3 cities</b> (70% of urban population): open stores aggressively there. Matching competitors' engagement adds a one-off <b>+5%</b> ($7.00 → $7.35). Reaching +6.5% would need Fully Engaged <b>+5.75pp</b> and Engaged <b>+23pp</b> (30% → ~59%), which is very ambitious.",
  prompt:["McBurger Co is a multinational fast-food company, one of the oldest and most respected in the industry.","It has been trying to establish itself in China with varying success. It's one of the leaders, but growth has fallen below expectations.","Over the last five years, it and its competitors faced several food safety scandals, but minimized the damage and kept sales. The CEO feels the firm is ready to focus on growth again.","You were hired to help the CEO identify what actions McBurger Co can take to grow faster in China."],
  clarify:[
    ["What are the objectives?","Get back to sustainable long-run revenue growth, and at least 8% a year to match the industry."],
    ["What do operations look like?","2,000 stores, 80% in Tier 1 cities, all owned and operated by the company. The target is 'Mainstream' customers. The menu is similar worldwide with some local adaptations."],
    ["How big is the market?","Fast food in China made $136B in Y0, growing 8.3%. Over the last 5 years it grew 10.1% a year."],
    ["What are city tiers?","Chinese cities are grouped by tier: Tier 1 are the largest and most prosperous, Tier 4 the smallest and least advanced."]
  ],
  openListen:["Asks for the growth target (≥8% a year)","Asks where the stores are (80% Tier 1)","Asks about market size and growth"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"3 min",
     say:["What factors would you want to consider when thinking about this problem?"],
     steer:["Original case: share the additional information (objectives, operations, market) as the candidate asks; it's all in the clarifying answers."],
     listen:["Market: size and growth by segment; changing preferences","Share: competitors and our positioning","Growth plan: stores, offering, brand, price, resources"],
     check:["Market: size and growth by region, food type, price point","Market: changing customer preferences","Share: competitors (segments, share, foreign/local, formats)","Share: our position in fast-growing segments","Share: competitiveness (value, product, brand)","Plan: store openings (locations, formats)","Plan: products and services","Plan: brand and marketing","Plan: price and revenue","Plan: resources and costs"],
     probe:["\"Where could new customers come from?\"","\"How would you compare yourself with competitors?\""],
     key:"<p><b>1. Market</b>: size and growth by segment (region, food type, price); changing customer preferences. <b>2. Share</b>: competitors (segments, share, offering, formats); our positioning in fast-growing segments and competitiveness. <b>3. Growth plan</b>: store openings, offering, brand and marketing, price, resources and costs.</p>"},
    {name:"Income distribution", time:"5 min",
     say:["McBurger Co has shared this exhibit on changes in income distribution in China. What can you conclude?"],
     give:{when:"Right away (Exhibit 1).",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 1.</b> Income distribution by city tier (% of population)</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Segment</th><th>Y-5</th><th>Y0</th><th>Y+5</th></tr><tr><td rowspan="4"><b>Tier 1</b><br><span class="muted">13 cities · 27% of urban GDP · 15% of urban pop.</span></td><td>Affluent</td><td>2</td><td>5</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>Mainstream</td><td>6</td><td>24</td><td>71</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>88</td><td>67</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>Poor</td><td>4</td><td>3</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="4"><b>Tier 2</b><br><span class="muted">35 cities · 35% of GDP · 26% of pop.</span></td><td>Affluent</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Mainstream</td><td>1</td><td>5</td><td>67</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>88</td><td>86</td><td>22</td></tr><tr><td>Poor</td><td>11</td><td>6</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="4"><b>Tier 3</b><br><span class="muted">369 cities · 33% of GDP · 45% of pop.</span></td><td>Affluent</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Mainstream</td><td>0</td><td>3</td><td>43</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>75</td><td>81</td><td>44</td></tr><tr><td>Poor</td><td>25</td><td>15</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="4"><b>Tier 4</b><br><span class="muted">188 cities · 5% of GDP · 14% of pop.</span></td><td>Affluent</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Mainstream</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td>53</td><td>64</td><td>56</td></tr><tr><td>Poor</td><td>47</td><td>33</td><td>17</td></tr></table></div><p class="muted">Read from stacked bar charts; some columns round to 99–101%.</p>`},
     listen:["Mainstream roughly triples in Tier 1 (24% → 71%), where McBurger is","Tier 1: more visits and spend per visit, plus new stores","Tier 2 and 3 (~70% of urban population): Mainstream becomes the majority or near it","Opportunity to expand into Tier 2–3","Next steps: link income to fast-food spend; map stores vs. income; test Tier 2–3 feasibility"],
     probe:["\"Where is our target customer going to be in 5 years?\"","\"Why do Tier 2 and 3 matter so much?\""],
     ifwrong:[["Focuses on Tier 4","\"How much of urban GDP and population is Tier 4?\""]],
     key:"<p>Very fast growth ahead: the <b>Mainstream segment roughly triples in Tier 1</b> (24% → 71%), where McBurger is established: drive more visits and spend per visit, and open stores to meet demand. <b>Tier 2 and 3</b> hold ~70% of the urban population, and Mainstream becomes the majority (or close to it) there: a major expansion opportunity. Next: income vs. fast-food spending, current store locations vs. income concentration, feasibility of Tier 2–3 expansion.</p>",
     stuck:["\"Which segment is McBurger targeting? How does it change?\""]},
    {name:"Engagement impact", time:"5 min",
     say:["The client asked us to look at customer engagement with the brand. The team believes that driving more customers to the restaurants will increase engagement, with a big impact on sales. Exhibit 2 shows the client's and competitors' engagement distributions and the spend per segment. What would be the impact on revenue if the client attained the same engagement distribution as its competitors?"],
     steer:["Original case: push the candidate to calculate a relative (%) increase in revenue."],
     give:{when:"Right away (Exhibit 2).",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 2.</b> Customer engagement and spending</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Segment</th><th>Client</th><th>Competitors</th><th>Avg. monthly spend per customer</th></tr><tr><td>Fully Engaged</td><td>10%</td><td>20%</td><td>$9</td></tr><tr><td>Engaged</td><td>20%</td><td>25%</td><td>$8</td></tr><tr><td>Not Engaged</td><td>50%</td><td>40%</td><td>$7</td></tr><tr><td>Actively Disengaged</td><td>20%</td><td>15%</td><td>$5</td></tr></table></div><p class="muted"><b>Fully Engaged</b>: strong emotional attachment, won't accept substitutes, brand ambassadors. <b>Engaged</b>: attached but can be tempted by a better offer. <b>Not Engaged</b>: brand-agnostic. <b>Actively Disengaged</b>: detached, switch easily.</p>`},
     num:{value:5, tol:.05, unit:"% revenue increase"},
     listen:["Current average spend: 10%×9 + 20%×8 + 50%×7 + 20%×5 = $7.00","Change: +10pp×9 + 5pp×8 − 10pp×7 − 5pp×5 = +$0.35","+5% revenue","One-off; doesn't deliver 8% a year alone","But more engagement → word of mouth, higher sales per store, winning bids for the best sites"],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">Current average spend = 10%×$9 + 20%×$8 + 50%×$7 + 20%×$5 = $7.00\nChange = (+10%)×9 + (+5%)×8 + (−10%)×7 + (−5%)×5 = +$0.35\n% change = 0.35 ÷ 7 = +5%</p><p>A one-off +5%, not a recurring 8% a year. But higher engagement drives word of mouth and sales per store, which helps win bids for the best new locations.</p>",
     stuck:["\"What does an average customer spend today?\""]},
    {name:"Engagement target", time:"5 min",
     say:["Based on experience, for every 0.5 percentage point increase in the share of 'Fully Engaged' customers, 'Engaged' rises by 2pp, while 'Not Engaged' and 'Actively Disengaged' fall by 2pp and 0.5pp. By how many percentage points would the Fully Engaged and Engaged shares have to rise to increase sales by 6.5%?"],
     num:{value:5.75, tol:.06, unit:"pp increase in Fully Engaged"},
     listen:["Target change in spend: $7 × 6.5% ≈ $0.46","Let Δ = change in Fully Engaged; Engaged +4Δ; Not Engaged −4Δ; Disengaged −Δ","9Δ + 32Δ − 28Δ − 5Δ = 8Δ = 0.46 → Δ ≈ 5.75pp","Fully Engaged ~16%; Engaged ~43%","Engaged + Fully Engaged doubles (~30% → ~59%): very ambitious","Next: did any competitor achieve this? What drives engagement?"],
     ifwrong:[["Sets the ratio as 1:2 instead of 1:4","\"Fully Engaged moves 0.5pp for every 2pp of Engaged. What's the ratio?\""]],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">Target change = $7 × 6.5% ≈ $0.46\n9Δ + 8(4Δ) + 7(−4Δ) + 5(−Δ) = 0.46\n9Δ + 32Δ − 28Δ − 5Δ = 8Δ = 0.46 → Δ ≈ 5.75pp\nFully Engaged: 10% → ~16% · Engaged: 20% + 23pp → ~43%</p><p>Engaged + Fully Engaged would need to double, from 30% to about 60%: extremely ambitious. Check whether any competitor has done this, and study what drives engagement.</p>",
     stuck:["\"Write each segment's change in terms of the Fully Engaged change.\"","\"What change in average spend is 6.5%?\""]},
    {name:"Engagement ideas", time:"3 min",
     say:["The client is looking for ideas to improve customer engagement. What would you suggest?"],
     listen:["Organizes ideas: loyalty, satisfaction, emotional attachment","Concrete examples in each"],
     check:["Loyalty: reward programs","Loyalty: products and services not found elsewhere","Satisfaction: excellent product quality","Satisfaction: outstanding customer experience","Satisfaction: fair prices","Attachment: iconic brand people want to belong to","Attachment: personal relationships (staff, community events)","Attachment: small acts of kindness (free coffee)"],
     key:"<p>Goal: loyal, satisfied customers who promote the brand and feel attached to it. <b>Loyalty</b>: rewards, exclusive products. <b>Satisfaction</b>: quality, customer experience, fair prices. <b>Emotional attachment</b>: an iconic brand, personal relationships with staff and community, small acts of kindness.</p>"},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["The CEO is about to talk to the Board and wants to know what your team has found so far."],
     listen:["Restates the goal: ≥8% a year growth","Answer: open stores aggressively in Tiers 1–3, riding the Mainstream boom","Tier 1 Mainstream triples; Tier 2–3 (70% of pop.) becoming Mainstream","Engagement gap: catching up = one-off +5%; matters for sales per store and site bids","Next: store opening plan, test engagement ideas"],
     key:"<p>\"You asked how McBurger can reach 8% annual growth. Our Mainstream focus positions us to beat that if we open stores aggressively in the first three tiers of cities. The Mainstream segment will roughly triple in Tier 1, where we're established, and become most of the population in Tiers 2 and 3, which hold about 70% of urban China, where we're not yet present. We also lag competitors on engagement, which hurts sales per store and our ability to win the best sites; catching up adds a one-off 5%, through loyalty, satisfaction and emotional attachment. Next: draw up a store-opening plan and test the engagement ideas.\"</p>"}
  ]
},
{
  id:"nurvaccine", title:"NUR Vaccine", type:"Public sector strategy", diff:"Medium", mins:30,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["Strategy"], sectors:["Healthcare","Public sector"], stretch:["Judgement & insights","Structuring"], video:false, rating:null, downloads:null},
  plain:"A newly elected Nigerian governor wants to raise child vaccination from 25% to 40% with a one-off $5M budget. The candidate frames supply vs. demand, compares four cities, costs a $15 cash incentive in the two best cities (it misses the target and the budget), and designs a mobile vaccination team.",
  bottom:"Prioritize <b>City B</b> (13% vaccinated, +120% response) and <b>City C</b> (28%, +40%). But the incentive there costs <b>$5.7M</b> (over the $5M budget) for 380k new vaccinations, reaching only <b>~32%</b>, short of 40%, and it doesn't fix root causes. Don't fund it as is: consider a smaller version in City B, and explore the mobile team.",
  prompt:["Our client is the newly elected governor of a region in Nigeria. The region faces serious health challenges, especially low child vaccination rates against the NUR disease.","Only 25% of the 5 million eligible children (aged 5 to 15) are vaccinated.","The governor has secured a one-off $5 million budget and wants to raise vaccination coverage to 40%.","How can the governor increase child vaccination rates?"],
  clarify:[
    ["How is the region organized?","There are 4 main cities, each with different existing coverage and different responsiveness to incentives."],
    ["How wealthy is the region?","It's economically underdeveloped: average income is under $60 a month."],
    ["Is the budget recurring?","No, it's a one-off $5 million."],
    ["What's the target?","40% coverage, up from 25%."]
  ],
  openListen:["Plays back: 25% → 40% of 5M children, with a one-off $5M","Asks how the region is split (4 cities)","Asks about income levels"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"3 min",
     say:["What factors would you consider to help the governor find solutions to increase the vaccination rate?"],
     listen:["Demand: can families afford it; do they know, trust and accept it","Supply: centers, doses, nurses, storage and distribution"],
     check:["Demand: can families afford vaccination?","Demand: is the vaccine state-funded?","Demand: social security reimbursement?","Demand: awareness of the vaccine","Demand: belief in its effectiveness","Demand: comfort and perceived safety","Supply: enough vaccination centers, functional and good quality","Supply: enough doses","Supply: enough nursing staff","Supply: storage and distribution facilities"],
     probe:["\"Is the problem that families don't come, or that there's nowhere to go?\""],
     key:"<p><b>1. Increase demand</b>: financial (affordability, state funding, reimbursement); culture and education (awareness, belief in effectiveness, perceived safety). <b>2. Increase supply</b>: vaccination centers (enough, functional), doses, nursing staff, storage and distribution.</p>"},
    {name:"Which cities?", time:"5 min",
     say:["The governor is considering a $15 cash incentive to families for each newly vaccinated child. We've estimated the program's impact in Exhibits 1 and 2. What's the current vaccination rate in each city? Which cities would you prioritize for this initiative?"],
     steer:["Original case: the candidate should calculate current rates per city but doesn't need the program's exact impact yet (that's the next question).","A strong candidate suggests why responsiveness differs between cities; if not, ask them to."],
     give:{when:"Right away (Exhibits 1 and 2).",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 1.</b> Distribution of eligible children (aged 5 to 15)</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>City</th><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>C</th><th>D</th></tr><tr><td>Eligible children</td><td>2.0M</td><td>1.5M</td><td>1.25M</td><td>0.25M</td></tr></table></div><p><b>Exhibit 2.</b> Unvaccinated eligible children and forecast impact of the program</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>City</th><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>C</th><th>D</th></tr><tr><td>Unvaccinated children</td><td>1.3M</td><td>1.3M</td><td>0.9M</td><td>0.2M</td></tr><tr><td>Forecast impact on number of children vaccinated</td><td>+10%</td><td>+120%</td><td>+40%</td><td>+30%</td></tr></table></div>`},
     listen:["A: (2 − 1.3) ÷ 2 = 35%","B: 0.2 ÷ 1.5 = 13%","C: 0.35 ÷ 1.25 = 28%","D: 0.05 ÷ 0.25 = 20%","Prioritize B (lowest rate, large, highest response) and C; deprioritize A (low response) and D (small)","Drivers: access and infrastructure, income, trust and culture, operational barriers"],
     check:["Driver: access and infrastructure (urban hubs have more clinics)","Driver: economic context ($15 matters more in poorer cities)","Driver: trust, awareness, culture","Driver: operational barriers (distance, hours, parents' time)"],
     probe:["\"Why might City A respond so little to cash?\""],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">A: 2 − 1.3 = 0.7M → 35%\nB: 1.5 − 1.3 = 0.2M → 13%\nC: 1.25 − 0.9 = 0.35M → 28%\nD: 0.25 − 0.2 = 0.05M → 20%</p><p><b>Prioritize City B</b> (lowest rate, ~30% of children, +120% response) and <b>City C</b> (+40%, many unvaccinated). City A responds weakly (+10%); City D is only 5% of children. Why responses differ: access and infrastructure, income (where $15 matters more), trust and culture, operational barriers.</p>",
     stuck:["\"How many children in City A are vaccinated today?\""]},
    {name:"Impact and cost", time:"4 min",
     say:["What would be the impact and cost of launching the program in City B and City C?"],
     steer:["Original case: the candidate can reuse the previous calculations."],
     num:{value:5.7e6, tol:.03, unit:"$ total cost"},
     listen:["B: 0.2M × 120% = 240k new → × $15 = $3.6M","C: 0.35M × 40% = 140k new → × $15 = $2.1M","380k new; 1.25M + 0.38M ≈ 1.6M → ~32%, short of 40%","$5.7M > $5M budget","One-off cash doesn't fix underlying supply or demand issues"],
     ifwrong:[["Applies the % to unvaccinated children","\"The impact is on the number of children vaccinated today.\""]],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">City B: 0.2M × 120% = 240k × $15 = $3.6M\nCity C: 0.35M × 40% = 140k × $15 = $2.1M\nTotal: 380k children for $5.7M\nCoverage: (1.25M + 0.38M) ÷ 5M ≈ 32%</p><p>Short of the 40% target and over the $5M budget, leaving nothing for other cities; a one-time payment doesn't address root causes.</p>",
     stuck:["\"How many children are vaccinated in City B today?\""]},
    {name:"Mobile team", time:"3 min",
     say:["The governor is also considering a mobile vaccination team traveling to rural areas with very low vaccination. Beyond financial incentives, what can he do to maximize the number of vaccinations this team gives?"],
     listen:["Education and awareness","Easy access","Community and local partnerships","Operational improvements"],
     check:["Talks with local leaders, teachers, religious figures","Local radio and loudspeakers with trusted testimonials","Visits at schools, markets, churches","Flexible hours around farming and work","Train mothers, elders, teachers as advocates","Partner with NGOs, health missions, local businesses","Peer outreach: families bring neighbors","Village announcements and SMS reminders","Pre-visit sign-up lists via community leaders"],
     key:"<p><b>Education and awareness</b>: community talks with trusted local figures; radio and testimonials. <b>Easy access</b>: go where people already gather; flexible hours. <b>Community partnerships</b>: local advocates, NGOs and businesses, peer outreach. <b>Operations</b>: announcements and SMS reminders, pre-visit sign-ups to manage flow and supply.</p>"},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["What is your final recommendation to the client?"],
     listen:["Answer first: don't spend the $5M on the $15 incentive in B and C as designed","$5.7M → only ~32%, two cities, over budget","Short-term boost, not a root-cause fix","Option: smaller-scale version in City B","Next: other uses of the budget; mobile team impact"],
     key:"<p>\"You asked how to raise child vaccination against NUR. I wouldn't spend the $5M on the $15 cash incentive in Cities B and C: it would cost $5.7M, over budget, and only lift coverage to about 32%, short of 40%, in just two cities. It's a short-term boost that doesn't solve the underlying problem. A smaller version in City B, where it creates the most momentum, could make sense. Next: look for more efficient, longer-term uses of the budget, and assess the mobile vaccination team.\"</p>"}
  ]
},
{
  id:"diagnosisai", title:"Diagnosis.AI", type:"Profitability, AI", diff:"Medium", mins:30,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["Profitability","AI"], sectors:["Healthcare"], stretch:["Judgement & insights"], video:false, rating:null, downloads:null},
  plain:"A US AI start-up reads medical scans and sells unlimited-use annual licenses. It grows fast but loses money. The candidate builds a cost/revenue tree, finds that heavy users (imaging centers, hospitals) cost more than they pay, benchmarks competitors with different pricing models, and finds fixes without abandoning unlimited licenses.",
  bottom:"Loss of <b>$13.9M</b>: Primary Care +$3.6M, Hospitals −$5.5M, Imaging Centers −$12M. Heavy users do 6–18× the scans but pay only 2.5–7× the revenue: <b>unlimited use is overused</b>. Competitors on per-scan or tiered pricing break even but grow slower. Keep unlimited licenses, but add <b>efficiency nudges, premium tiers priced by segment, and cost cuts</b>.",
  prompt:["Your client is Diagnosis.AI, a US medical technology start-up specializing in AI-powered interpretation of medical scans.","It serves three B2B segments: diagnostic imaging centers, hospitals and primary care centers. It sells licenses: unlimited usage for a fixed annual fee.","This simple offer has driven fast revenue growth and scale, but the company hasn't reached breakeven, which increasingly worries investors.","Diagnosis.AI wants to reach breakeven while keeping its fast growth. How would you recommend they do this?"],
  clarify:[
    ["What does the tool do?","It analyzes an image and gives a diagnosis or recommends more scans or tests."],
    ["Is there a target?","No target beyond breakeven with the current product."],
    ["How is pricing set?","Licenses are sold B2B with different price tiers for Primary Care Centers, Hospitals and Imaging Centers. Each license has unlimited usage."],
    ["Where do they operate?","Only the US, with no plans to expand internationally."]
  ],
  openListen:["Plays back: reach breakeven without losing growth","Asks about the pricing model (unlimited licenses by segment)","Asks about geography and product scope"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"3 min",
     say:["What factors would you consider to answer the client's question?"],
     listen:["Cost side: cost per scan, number of scans, fixed costs","Revenue side: revenue per client, number of clients","Links scan volume to the unlimited-use model"],
     check:["Cost per scan: more efficient inference (power, GPUs)","Cost per scan: triage model for simple cases","Scan volume: usage caps at peak times","Scan volume: quality filters to reject unclear images","Fixed: faster, incremental R&D","Fixed: cheaper training compute (open-source, partnerships, synthetic data)","Fixed: efficient testing and validation","Fixed: review overheads","Revenue per client: premium tier","Revenue per client: upsell new high-margin products","Revenue per client: custom support and integrations","Revenue per client: raise license price","More clients: grow existing (higher-paying) segments","More clients: new higher-margin segments (specialty clinics, nursing homes)"],
     probe:["\"With unlimited usage, what drives your costs?\""],
     key:"<p><b>1. Cut costs</b>: cost per scan (efficient inference, triage model), number of scans (usage caps, quality filters), fixed costs (R&D focus, training compute, testing, overheads). <b>2. Increase revenue</b>: revenue per client (premium tier, upsells, custom products, price increases) and more clients (existing and new, higher-margin segments).</p>"},
    {name:"Profit by segment", time:"5 min",
     say:["Using Exhibit 1, calculate Diagnosis.AI's baseline profitability, overall and by client segment. What is causing the profitability problem?"],
     steer:["Original case: no other costs to worry about for now. The Board has ruled marketing costs out of scope, as they're in line with competitors."],
     give:{when:"Right away (Exhibit 1).",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 1.</b> Performance by client segment (Year 0)</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Segment</th><th>License revenue ($M)</th><th>Number of scans (M)</th><th>Cost per scan ($)</th><th>Fixed cost ($M)</th></tr><tr><td>Primary Care Network</td><td>10</td><td>0.5</td><td>8.75</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Hospitals</td><td>24</td><td>3</td><td>8.5</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging Centers</td><td>70</td><td>9</td><td>8</td><td>10</td></tr></table></div>`},
     num:{value:-13.9e6, tol:.03, unit:"$ total profit"},
     listen:["Profit = revenue − scans × cost per scan − fixed cost","Primary Care: 10 − 4.4 − 2 = +$3.6M","Hospitals: 24 − 25.5 − 4 = −$5.5M","Imaging: 70 − 72 − 10 = −$12M","Total ≈ −$13.9M","Cause: heavy usage. Imaging does 18× the scans of Primary Care for 7× the revenue; Hospitals 6× for 2.4×","Next: is this market-wide? Look at competitors"],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">Primary Care: 10 − (0.5 × 8.75 = 4.4) − 2 = +$3.6M\nHospitals: 24 − (3 × 8.5 = 25.5) − 4 = −$5.5M\nImaging: 70 − (9 × 8 = 72) − 10 = −$12M\nTotal ≈ −$13.9M</p><p>Imaging Centers, the largest segment, lose the most; Hospitals lose too; Primary Care is profitable. The cause is scan volume: Imaging does 18× Primary Care's scans for 7× the revenue, Hospitals 6× for ~2.5×. Unlimited licenses are overused at today's prices. Next: check competitors.</p>",
     stuck:["\"What's the total cost of scans for Hospitals?\""]},
    {name:"Competitor benchmark", time:"4 min",
     say:["Diagnosis.AI has three main competitors. A charges a small fee per scan. B uses a hybrid: some clients pay per scan, others have tiered licenses with usage limits. C copies Diagnosis.AI: unlimited licenses for a fixed fee. Exhibit 2 shows their quarterly revenue last year; Exhibit 3 their non-marketing costs. How does Diagnosis.AI compare, and what does that say about its profitability problem and possible fixes?"],
     give:{when:"Right away (Exhibits 2 and 3).",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 2.</b> Quarterly revenue, Year 0 ($M)</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th></th><th>Q1</th><th>Q2</th><th>Q3</th><th>Q4</th></tr><tr><td>Diagnosis.AI</td><td>20</td><td>24</td><td>28</td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td>Competitor A</td><td>23</td><td>22</td><td>24</td><td>26</td></tr><tr><td>Competitor B</td><td>16</td><td>17</td><td>18</td><td>21</td></tr><tr><td>Competitor C</td><td>6</td><td>7</td><td>7</td><td>8</td></tr></table></div><p><b>Exhibit 3.</b> Quarterly non-marketing costs, Year 0 ($M)</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th></th><th>Q1</th><th>Q2</th><th>Q3</th><th>Q4</th></tr><tr><td>Diagnosis.AI</td><td>22</td><td>28</td><td>32</td><td>36</td></tr><tr><td>Competitor A</td><td>24</td><td>23</td><td>24</td><td>23</td></tr><tr><td>Competitor B</td><td>16</td><td>17</td><td>16</td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td>Competitor C</td><td>8</td><td>9</td><td>10</td><td>12</td></tr></table></div><p class="muted">Read from stacked bar charts.</p>`},
     listen:["Diagnosis.AI is the leader and grows fastest (20 → 32)","A, and to a lesser extent B, are in a 3-way race","Unlimited model = growth advantage","But A and B roughly break even; Diagnosis.AI's cost share exceeds its revenue share","C (same model) also loses money: the model hurts profitability"],
     probe:["\"What do the two unlimited-license players have in common?\""],
     key:"<p>Diagnosis.AI is the <b>market leader and grows fastest</b> (20 → 32 a quarter); A and, less so, B are in a three-way race. So the unlimited model helps growth. But A and B <b>break even</b>, while Diagnosis.AI's share of industry cost exceeds its share of revenue. Competitor C, with the same unlimited model, also loses money: <b>the unlimited model is hurting profitability</b>.</p>",
     stuck:["\"Compare each player's revenue with its costs.\""]},
    {name:"Keeping unlimited licenses", time:"4 min",
     say:["You tell the CEO the losses come from very high scan volumes per user in imaging centers and hospitals, and that Diagnosis.AI is less profitable than competitors using pay-per-use or tiered licenses. The CEO is firmly committed to the single-price, unlimited license: changing it could hurt the brand and slow growth. Within that constraint, how could it reach profitability?"],
     steer:["Original case: if needed, steer the candidate to work within the single-price unlimited license and how it can be flexed. Pay-per-use is not an option."],
     listen:["Encourage client efficiency","Charge more for more value (by segment, premium packages)","Reduce costs"],
     check:["Soft limits with nudges (alerts after X scans per user)","Co-develop features that flag wasted scans","Cashback for lower usage","Premium packages for imaging centers","Charge extra for high-usage features (multi-site integration)","Segment pricing: higher licenses (with features) for imaging and hospitals","Lower cost per scan (efficiency, outsource non-core)","Lower fixed costs (R&D, training compute, overheads)"],
     ifwrong:[["Suggests pay-per-scan","\"The CEO has ruled that out. How can you flex the unlimited license instead?\""]],
     key:"<p><b>Encourage efficiency</b>: soft limits with nudges, features that flag wasted scans, cashback for lower use. <b>Charge more for more value</b>: premium packages for imaging centers, extras for high-usage features like multi-site integration, higher segment prices with special features. <b>Reduce costs</b>: cost per scan and fixed costs.</p>"},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["The CEO has a quarterly investor update next week. What findings and emerging recommendations can be presented at this point?"],
     listen:["Problem located in Imaging Centers and Hospitals: overuse under unlimited licenses","Less profitable than competitors, but faster growing","Within the constraint: efficiency incentives, segment premium offers, cost cuts","Risk: may not fully close the gap","Next: talk to customers and sales team about price rises; review cost savings"],
     key:"<p>\"Diagnosis.AI is struggling with profitability. The problem is Imaging Centers and, to a lesser extent, Hospitals: they use 18× and 6× the scans of profitable Primary Care, but pay only 7× and 2.5× as much, because unlimited licenses get overused. That makes us less profitable than competitors, though they're slower-growing and smaller, which is why we're keeping the unlimited model. Within it, efficiency incentives, higher-priced premium offers by segment and targeted cost cuts can get us to breakeven. The risk is that it isn't enough. Next: test price rises with customers and our sales team, and size the cost savings.\"</p>"}
  ]
}
);
Object.assign(window.CASE_ADDITIONS,{
  quantumbank:{clarify:[3]},
  mcburger:{},
  nurvaccine:{clarify:[2,3]},
  diagnosisai:{}
});
Object.assign(window.CASE_SCRIPTS,{
quantumbank:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me make sure I understand.</b> Quantum Bank, one of Europe's largest banks, wants to know whether a large-scale AI automation program in Retail Banking makes sense, mainly to cut costs. <b>The COO wants an assessment of the opportunity.</b> Could I ask how big Retail Banking is, what share of costs it carries, and whether there's a budget or target?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd assess it in three steps.</b> <b>First, which use cases suit AI</b>: high-volume, repetitive processes with clear rules, where failure isn't catastrophic, and where peers already succeed. <b>Second, the financial impact</b>: labor and error savings, any revenue uplift from faster approvals, against the investment and payback. <b>Third, readiness</b>: is our data available, clean and governed, and do we have leadership buy-in, talent, change management and AI governance? <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Where AI fits":"<p><b>I'd go area by area and judge each on volume, rules and risk.</b> <b>Customer service</b> is the easiest win: chatbots, smart call routing, email triage. <b>Credit and loans</b>: document checks and instant pre-approvals. <b>Fraud</b>: real-time anomaly detection, as long as models are explainable. <b>Compliance</b>: KYC and watchlist screening, carefully, given regulatory scrutiny. <b>Back office</b>: scheduling, ATM maintenance, process automation. <b>I'd start where volume is high and risk is low.</b></p>",
  "Moonshot ideas":"<p><b>Three ideas that go beyond efficiency.</b> <b>A personal AI banker</b> for every customer, which advises, simulates decisions and even negotiates rates. <b>An AI life-stage concierge</b> that anticipates a new baby or home purchase and offers pre-approved bundles. And <b>synthetic customer personas</b> to test new products and lending policies before launch, so we innovate faster and more safely.</p>",
  "Budget allocation":"<p><b>Two things matter: return per euro, and whether a project can be partly funded.</b> Returns are: Loan Review 2.0, Compliance and Fraud 1.75, Chatbot 1.67, Forecasting 1.13. <b>I'd fully fund Loan Review</b>, €5M for €10M. With €5M left, <b>Fraud Monitoring beats Compliance</b>: it uses €4M for €7M, and the last €1M goes into the Chatbot, which is linear, for €1.67M. <b>Total: about €18.7M a year on €10M.</b> Picking Compliance instead only gets to €18.5M. <b>So what: ranking by ROI alone misleads when projects are all-or-nothing.</b></p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>Invest the full €10M in three projects: Loan Review, Fraud Monitoring, and part of the Chatbot.</b> That saves about €18.7M a year, nearly 2× the budget. Two projects deliver over 90% of it and can start now. Loan Review is sensitive, so it needs strong governance and early talks with regulators. <b>Next</b>: assign owners and targets, set up a central AI team, reassess the other use cases after the first wave, and start exploring moonshots.</p>"
 }},
mcburger:{
 open:"<p><b>To recap</b>: McBurger is a global fast-food leader whose growth in China has fallen short, after a few years spent managing food safety scandals. <b>The CEO wants to know how to grow faster.</b> Could I ask what growth target we're aiming for, where the stores are, and how the market is growing?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd look at three areas.</b> <b>The market</b>: how big it is and which segments grow fastest, by region, food type and price, and how preferences are shifting. <b>Our share</b>: who the competitors are, and how well we're positioned in the fast-growing segments on value, product and brand. <b>The growth plan</b>: where to open stores, what to offer, brand, pricing, and the resources it needs. <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Income distribution":"<p><b>The headline: our target customer is about to multiply.</b> In Tier 1, where we are, Mainstream goes from 24% to about 71% of the population in five years: roughly triple. So we should get more visits and spend from existing stores and open new ones. <b>The bigger prize is Tiers 2 and 3</b>: they hold about 70% of the urban population, and Mainstream becomes most of it. <b>Next</b>, I'd check how income links to fast-food spending, map our stores against income, and test expansion into Tiers 2 and 3.</p>",
  "Engagement impact":"<p><b>I'll compare average spend per customer.</b> Today: 10% at $9, 20% at $8, 50% at $7 and 20% at $5, so <b>$7.00</b>. With competitors' mix, we gain 10 points at $9 and 5 at $8, and lose 10 at $7 and 5 at $5: <b>plus 35 cents, or 5%</b>. <b>So what</b>: it's a one-off 5%, not 8% a year. But engagement also drives word of mouth and sales per store, which helps us win the best new locations.</p>",
  "Engagement target":"<p><b>6.5% of $7 is about 46 cents.</b> If Fully Engaged rises by Δ, Engaged rises 4Δ, Not Engaged falls 4Δ and Disengaged falls Δ. So 9Δ plus 32Δ minus 28Δ minus 5Δ, which is <b>8Δ, equals 0.46</b>, and <b>Δ is about 5.75 points</b>. Fully Engaged goes to about 16% and Engaged to about 43%. <b>That doubles our engaged customers from 30% to nearly 60%, which is very ambitious.</b> I'd check whether any competitor has done it.</p>",
  "Engagement ideas":"<p><b>I'd build engagement on three things.</b> <b>Loyalty</b>: a rewards program and products you can't get elsewhere. <b>Satisfaction</b>: great food, a great experience, fair prices. <b>Emotional attachment</b>: a brand people want to belong to, staff who know their regulars, community events, and small surprises like a free coffee.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>You asked how to reach 8% growth. Our Mainstream focus lets us beat it if we open stores aggressively in the first three tiers.</b> Mainstream will triple in Tier 1, where we're strong, and become most of Tiers 2 and 3, home to 70% of urban China. <b>We also lag on engagement</b>: catching up is a one-off 5%, but it matters for sales per store and winning sites. <b>Next</b>: a store-opening plan, and pilots of loyalty and experience ideas.</p>"
 }},
nurvaccine:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me play that back.</b> Only 25% of 5 million eligible children are vaccinated against NUR, and the governor has a one-off $5M <b>to reach 40%</b>, about 750,000 more children. Could I ask how the region is organized, how wealthy families are, and what's been tried before?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>Vaccination needs both families who want it and the means to deliver it, so I'd split demand and supply.</b> <b>Demand</b>: can families afford it, is it free or reimbursed, and do they know about the vaccine, trust it and feel safe with it? <b>Supply</b>: are there enough working vaccination centers, doses and nurses, and the storage and distribution to reach every child? <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Which cities?":"<p><b>Current rates first.</b> City A: 0.7 of 2 million, <b>35%</b>. City B: 0.2 of 1.5, <b>13%</b>. City C: 0.35 of 1.25, <b>28%</b>. City D: 0.05 of 0.25, <b>20%</b>. <b>I'd prioritize City B</b>: the lowest rate, a large population and by far the strongest response, +120%. <b>Then City C</b>, at +40%. A barely responds and D is tiny. The differences probably come from access to clinics, income, since $15 matters more in poorer cities, and trust in health programs.</p>",
  "Impact and cost":"<p><b>City B</b>: 0.2 million vaccinated today, plus 120%, is <b>240,000</b> children, at $15 each, <b>$3.6M</b>. <b>City C</b>: 0.35 million plus 40% is <b>140,000</b>, <b>$2.1M</b>. Together, 380,000 children for <b>$5.7M</b>. Coverage goes from 1.25 to about 1.6 million, <b>32%</b>. <b>So what: we miss the 40% target and overspend the $5M</b>, and a one-off payment doesn't fix the root causes.</p>",
  "Mobile team":"<p><b>I'd tackle four areas.</b> <b>Awareness</b>: community talks with trusted local leaders, and local radio with real testimonials. <b>Access</b>: go to schools, markets and churches, at hours that suit farmers. <b>Partners</b>: train mothers and elders as advocates, and work with NGOs and local businesses. <b>Operations</b>: SMS and village announcements before each visit, and sign-up lists so we bring enough doses.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I wouldn't spend the $5M on the cash incentive in Cities B and C as designed.</b> It costs $5.7M, over budget, and only reaches about 32%, short of 40%, in two cities, without fixing the root causes. <b>A smaller pilot in City B</b>, where response is strongest, could still make sense. <b>Next</b>, I'd look for longer-lasting uses of the budget and size the mobile team's impact.</p>"
 }},
diagnosisai:{
 open:"<p><b>To recap</b>: Diagnosis.AI sells unlimited-use licenses for AI scan reading to imaging centers, hospitals and primary care. It's growing fast but isn't profitable. <b>The goal is breakeven without killing growth.</b> Could I ask what exactly the tool does, how pricing differs by segment, and whether there's any target beyond breakeven?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>Breakeven means closing the gap between revenue and cost, so I'd look at both.</b> <b>Costs</b>: the cost per scan, through more efficient computing or a cheaper triage model; the number of scans, through caps or quality filters; and fixed costs like R&D, training and overheads. <b>Revenue</b>: more per client, through premium tiers, upsells or price; and more clients, in existing or new segments. <b>With unlimited usage, I suspect scan volume is key.</b> <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Profit by segment":"<p><b>Profit is revenue minus scan costs minus fixed costs.</b> Primary Care: 10 minus 4.4 minus 2, <b>+$3.6M</b>. Hospitals: 24 minus 25.5 minus 4, <b>−$5.5M</b>. Imaging: 70 minus 72 minus 10, <b>−$12M</b>. <b>Overall, about −$13.9M.</b> <b>The cause is usage</b>: Imaging does 18× Primary Care's scans for 7× the revenue, and Hospitals 6× for about 2.5×. Unlimited licenses are being overused at today's prices. Next, I'd check whether competitors have the same problem.</p>",
  "Competitor benchmark":"<p><b>Two takeaways.</b> <b>We win on growth</b>: we're the leader and grew from 20 to 32 a quarter, faster than anyone, so unlimited licenses help sell. <b>But we lose on profit</b>: A and B, with per-scan or capped pricing, roughly break even, while our costs outgrow our revenue. And C, with our model, also loses money. <b>So the unlimited model is what's hurting profitability.</b></p>",
  "Keeping unlimited licenses":"<p><b>We can keep one unlimited license but change how it's used, priced and served.</b> <b>Nudge efficiency</b>: soft alerts after a certain number of scans, reports that flag wasted scans, cashback for lighter users. <b>Charge for value</b>: premium packages for imaging centers, extras like multi-site integration, and higher prices for heavy-use segments. <b>Cut costs</b>: cheaper inference per scan and lower fixed costs.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>The losses come from Imaging Centers and Hospitals overusing unlimited licenses</b>: 18× and 6× the scans of Primary Care for 7× and 2.5× the revenue. That makes us less profitable than per-scan competitors, though we grow faster, which is why we'll keep unlimited licenses. <b>Within that model, efficiency nudges, premium segment pricing and cost cuts</b> can get us to breakeven. <b>The risk</b> is that it's not enough. <b>Next</b>: test price rises with customers and size the cost savings.</p>"
 }}
});
