window.CASES.push(
{
  id:"patati", title:"Patati", type:"Turnaround", diff:"Medium", mins:30,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["Turnaround"], sectors:["Food & Beverage"], stretch:["Numeracy","Judgement & insights"], video:false, rating:4.6, downloads:9395},
  plain:"A French potato-fries maker launched potato wedges in the UK, USA and Japan; only the UK is declining. The candidate reads revenue by market, works out why volume fell with no price change, discovers competitors cut prices by growing potatoes locally, and decides whether a $3M investment to do the same pays off.",
  bottom:"UK revenue fell <b>25%</b> after a strong launch. Competitors dropped price $6 → $4 by <b>growing potatoes in UK greenhouses</b> (75% of cost is potatoes, transport and import duties). Investing $3M: revenue ~$37.5M → <b>~$7.5M profit</b>, vs. ~$22.3M → <b>~$4.5M</b> if Patati keeps declining. Roughly breaks even in the period and wins long term: <b>invest</b>.",
  prompt:["Patati is a French company selling raw material for potato fries. It's global and known for many varieties of fries.","Three years ago it launched a new potato wedges product in its top 3 markets: the UK, USA and Japan. It has done well in Japan and the USA, but profits have declined in the UK.","The CEO is concerned about the UK wedges business and wants you to analyze the situation and advise how to turn it around.","How should the client go about solving this problem?"],
  clarify:[
    ["How many wedge products do they sell?","Only one variety / brand of potato wedges."],
    ["Is the decline only in the UK?","Yes. All other markets are performing well."],
    ["What would success look like?","Bringing UK profits back to the level of 3 years ago."]
  ],
  openListen:["Plays back: UK wedge profits falling, other markets fine","Asks what success means (back to launch-year profit)","Asks about the product range"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"2–3 min",
     say:["How would you structure your approach to the question?"],
     listen:["Revenue: volume (market shrinking? losing share?) and price","Costs along the value chain: raw material, production, distribution, sales and marketing"],
     check:["Volume: is the UK wedges market shrinking?","Volume: are fewer customers buying ours?","Price: has our unit price fallen?","Cost: raw material","Cost: production","Cost: distribution","Cost: sales and marketing"],
     probe:["\"How would you tell a market problem from a Patati problem?\""],
     key:"<p><b>1. Revenue</b>: volume (is the UK market shrinking, or are fewer customers buying ours?) and price (has it fallen?). <b>2. Costs</b> anywhere in the value chain: raw material, production, distribution, sales and marketing.</p>"},
    {name:"Revenue by market", time:"3 min",
     say:["The client shares revenue data from the last 3 years. What insights can you draw from it?"],
     steer:["Original case: candidates naturally comment on the UK's steep decline, but they should also note the launch year was very successful, so something must have changed since."],
     give:{when:"Right away (Exhibit 1).",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 1.</b> Patati's potato wedges revenue ($M)</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th></th><th>Year 1</th><th>Year 2</th><th>Year 3</th></tr><tr><td>UK</td><td>7.98</td><td>6.85</td><td>5.98</td></tr><tr><td>USA</td><td>8.00</td><td>10.50</td><td>12.83</td></tr><tr><td>Japan</td><td>8.00</td><td>12.89</td><td>13.30</td></tr></table></div>`},
     num:{value:25, tol:.06, unit:"% UK decline, Year 1 to 3"},
     listen:["UK down ~25% from Year 1 to Year 3","Year 1 nearly equal across markets, though the UK is probably smallest: launch went well","From Year 2 something changed in the UK, while others grow fast","Hypothesis: e.g. competitor discounting"],
     key:"<p>UK revenue fell <b>25%</b> (7.98 → 5.98). But Year 1 was almost equal across all three markets, even though the UK is likely the smallest, so <b>the launch was a success</b>. From Year 2 the UK declined while the others grew fast: <b>something changed after launch</b>, e.g. competitors discounting.</p>",
     stuck:["\"How did the UK do in its first year compared with the others?\""]},
    {name:"Why volume fell", time:"3 min",
     say:["The client tells you our product's price hasn't changed. So what could explain the UK revenue decline after a strong launch year?"],
     listen:["Price unchanged → units must have fallen","Groups reasons: customers, competition, distribution, product, marketing"],
     check:["Customers: shift away from wedges (e.g. to regular fries)","Customers: shift to a wedge we don't offer (e.g. low-fat)","Competition: better or alternative products (e.g. crinkle-cut)","Competition: lower prices","Distribution: lost distributors","Distribution: lost shelf space","Product: quality issues or bad reviews","Product: portion size less attractive","Marketing: too little spend after launch","Marketing: wrong channels (TV vs. social)","Marketing: messaging not adapted to the UK"],
     probe:["\"Which of these would you check first, and how?\""],
     key:"<p>If price didn't change, <b>units fell</b>. Possible reasons: <b>customer preferences</b> (away from wedges, or toward a type we don't sell); <b>competition</b> (better products, lower prices); <b>distribution</b> (lost distributors or shelf space); <b>our product</b> (quality, reviews, portion size); <b>marketing</b> (too little, wrong channels, not localized).</p>"},
    {name:"How competitors cut price", time:"4 min",
     say:["Research suggests competitors cut prices per portion from $6 in Year 1 to $4 in Year 3 without significantly losing profitability. Our price stayed at $6. How do you think competitors made this possible? Feel free to ask for more information."],
     steer:["Original case: this is a discussion. Let the candidate suggest and debate ideas before sharing the information below."],
     give:{when:"After they've suggested ideas, or as they ask.",
       html:"<ul><li><b>75% of cost</b> is raw materials, transportation to the factory, and import duties for potatoes. Production, distribution and marketing make up the rest.</li><li>Wedges use a specific potato variety (broad in the middle, less starch), mostly imported into the UK from nearby countries.</li><li>Competitors have invested in <b>UK greenhouses</b> to grow this variety domestically, saving import duties and transport.</li><li>There's no other significant difference in how Patati makes wedges vs. competitors.</li></ul>"},
     listen:["Competitors must have changed their cost structure","Lists cost buckets: raw materials, production, transport, marketing, overhead","Focuses on the biggest bucket: potato sourcing","Concludes: imported vs. home-grown potatoes is the key cost gap"],
     key:"<p>Cutting price by a third without losing profitability means competitors <b>cut costs</b>, most likely in the biggest component. Since 75% of cost is potatoes, transport and import duties, they changed <b>sourcing</b>: growing the variety in UK greenhouses saves duties and transport. Patati's reliance on imported potatoes is the key cost disadvantage.</p>",
     stuck:["\"What's the biggest cost in making wedges?\""]},
    {name:"UK potato investment", time:"6 min",
     say:["Investing in UK potato farming costs $3M in R&D. Once Patati uses home-grown potatoes, price can fall $1 per portion each year down to a floor of $4. With every $1 drop, Patati expects to sell 500,000 more units a year. Margin stays at 20% as cost falls with price. Should Patati invest the $3M?"],
     steer:["Original case: if the candidate starts an NPV, tell them to ignore the time value of money and check whether the extra profit covers the investment.","If they need help: current volume = $6M sales ÷ $6 price ≈ 1M units. Suggest sketching a table and rounding."],
     give:{when:"If asked.", html:"<ul><li>The client wants to evaluate the next 4 years</li><li>If Patati keeps its current price, sales will decline about <b>15% a year</b></li></ul>"},
     num:{value:7.5e6, tol:.04, unit:"$ profit if Patati invests (Years 0–4)"},
     listen:["Compares invest vs. don't invest","Current volume ≈ 1M units ($6M ÷ $6)","Invest: prices 6, 5, 4, 4, 4; units 1, 1.5, 2, 2, 2 → $37.5M → ~$7.5M profit","Don't invest: −15% a year → ~$22.3M → ~$4.5M profit","$7.5M − $3M ≈ $4.5M: roughly breaks even in the period","Longer term: not investing means continued decline, likely exit"],
     ifwrong:[["Only computes the invest scenario","\"Compared with what? What happens if Patati does nothing?\""]],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">Invest:     Y0 $6×1M · Y1 $5×1.5M · Y2–4 $4×2M → 6 + 7.5 + 8 + 8 + 8 = $37.5M → ×20% ≈ $7.5M\nDon't invest: 1, 0.85, 0.72, 0.61, 0.52M units × $6 → 6 + 5.1 + 4.3 + 3.7 + 3.1 ≈ $22.3M → ≈ $4.5M\nInvest net of $3M ≈ $4.5M ≈ no-invest profit</p><p>Roughly breaks even within the period; over a longer horizon it's clearly better, since the alternative is continued decline and probably exiting UK wedges.</p>",
     stuck:["\"How many units does Patati sell today?\"","\"Sketch a table: year, price, units, revenue.\""]},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["What do you recommend to Patati?"],
     listen:["Answer first: invest and grow potatoes in the UK","Reasons: competitors do it successfully; import costs cause our price gap; investment beats decline","Next: build the capabilities and expertise needed","If more time: short-term demand boosters (e.g. advertising)"],
     key:"<p>\"Patati should invest to grow its own potatoes in the UK. All competitors do it successfully and have a cost advantage; our imported potatoes force a higher price that drives customers away; and the investment is clearly better than continued decline. Next, identify the capabilities and expertise needed and build a plan to source or develop them. With more time, I'd look at short-term ways to win back customers, like an innovative advertising campaign.\"</p>"}
  ]
},
{
  id:"lowcarbon", title:"Low Carbon Housing", type:"Sustainability", diff:"Medium", mins:30,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["Sustainability"], sectors:["Real Estate"], stretch:["Judgement & insights"], video:false, rating:null, downloads:null},
  plain:"A real estate developer is bidding to build a landmark low-emission residential district in the Middle East. The candidate structures emission levers across the building's life, sets criteria for picking greener materials, finds which low-carbon material to drop to keep costs within 110% of the baseline, and suggests ways to help residents cut emissions.",
  bottom:"Low-carbon materials cut material emissions <b>72,275 → 33,800 t CO₂</b> but raise cost <b>$23.6M → $28.3M</b> (cap: 110% ≈ $26M). <b>Keep mineral wool instead of hempcrete</b>: saves $2.8M (→ ~$25.5M) and loses only 225 t (&lt;1%). Emissions still fall <b>~50%</b>. Add resident initiatives (mobility, energy, awareness, circular economy).",
  prompt:["Your client is a real estate developer that wants to answer a Request for Proposal (RFP) to develop a new residential district in a Middle Eastern country. The district should be a landmark for environmental impact.","They want our support to be competitive in this RFP. The priority is to show how they'd minimize the project's greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions while keeping costs competitive with other bidders.","How could you support your client in achieving this objective?"],
  clarify:[
    ["Which emissions count?","Emissions during construction and during residents' use of the district, including emissions from suppliers. We can ignore the design phase and the end of the buildings' life."],
    ["Is there an emissions target?","No overall target. We'll discuss specific targets as we go."],
    ["Does the client build in the region?","No. It has no capabilities in the Middle East and would subcontract engineering, construction management and construction."],
    ["Only GHG, or other environmental impacts too?","Purely GHG emissions for now."]
  ],
  openListen:["Plays back: minimize GHG while staying cost-competitive in the bid","Asks which emissions count (construction, use, suppliers)","Asks about targets and the client's capabilities"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"3 min",
     say:["What drivers can our client consider to reduce the GHG emissions of this project?"],
     steer:["Original case: candidates who know GHG reporting may use Scope 1 (direct), Scope 2 (energy bought) and Scope 3 (suppliers and customers). For a generalist, a structure following the project's lifecycle is appropriate."],
     listen:["Lifecycle structure: construction vs. use (or Scopes 1–3)","Construction: materials, transport, process","Use: district energy, building design, renewables, transport, carbon capture","Keeps the cost constraint in view"],
     check:["Construction: lower-carbon materials","Construction: local sourcing, greener logistics","Construction: equipment, on-site energy","Use: district energy (e.g. underground links to cut cooling)","Use: efficient design (passive cooling, orientation, insulation, HVAC, smart meters)","Use: on-site solar","Use: EV charging, public transport","Use: carbon capture (nature- or tech-based)"],
     probe:["\"Where would you expect most emissions to come from?\""],
     key:"<p><b>1. During construction</b> (within cost limits): low-carbon materials, local sourcing and greener logistics, cleaner construction equipment and on-site energy. <b>2. During use</b>: district energy (shared cooling links), energy-efficient design (passive cooling, orientation, insulation, HVAC, smart meters), on-site renewables, sustainable transport (EV charging, public transport), carbon capture.</p>"},
    {name:"Material criteria", time:"3 min",
     say:["The client has already analyzed emission cuts from efficient design and construction processes. Now they want help reducing emissions by sourcing more sustainable materials. Which criteria would you use to select the most promising materials?"],
     listen:["Groups criteria: environmental, economic, practical, market and regulatory, social"],
     check:["Environment: emissions in production, transport, installation","Environment: renewability","Environment: energy efficiency","Environment: local availability","Economic: initial cost","Economic: lifecycle cost","Economic: scalability","Practical: performance and durability","Practical: compatibility","Practical: constructability","Market: supplier network","Regulatory: safety and building standards","Regulatory: government incentives","Social: aesthetics","Social: user comfort"],
     key:"<p><b>Environmental</b>: emissions across production, transport and installation; renewability; energy efficiency; local availability. <b>Economic</b>: initial and lifecycle cost, scalability. <b>Practical</b>: performance and durability, compatibility, constructability. <b>Market and regulatory</b>: supplier network, building standards, incentives. <b>Social</b>: aesthetics, comfort.</p>"},
    {name:"Materials within budget", time:"6 min",
     say:["The client shared the project's emission reductions using alternative materials. They're pleased with the reductions, but worried higher costs hurt competitiveness. Which low-carbon materials could be removed to keep costs under 110% of the baseline, and what would the impact be on cost and emissions?"],
     steer:["Original case: if the candidate goes line by line computing every total, prompt them to look for shortcuts. If they struggle, walk through the relative cost per ton of concrete vs. low-carbon concrete as an example."],
     give:{when:"Right away (Exhibit 1).",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 1.</b> Emissions and costs: baseline vs. low-carbon materials</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Baseline material</th><th>Mass (t)</th><th>Emission factor (kg CO₂/t)</th><th>Cost ($/t)</th></tr><tr><td>Concrete</td><td>90,000</td><td>450</td><td>110</td></tr><tr><td>Steel</td><td>15,000</td><td>1,900</td><td>700</td></tr><tr><td>Bricks</td><td>10,000</td><td>275</td><td>150</td></tr><tr><td>Mineral wool</td><td>7,500</td><td>70</td><td>225</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2"><b>Total</b></td><td><b>72,275 t CO₂</b></td><td><b>$23.6M</b></td></tr></table></div><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Low-carbon material</th><th>Mass (t)</th><th>Emission factor (kg CO₂/t)</th><th>Cost ($/t)</th></tr><tr><td>Low-carbon concrete</td><td>90,000</td><td>150</td><td>120</td></tr><tr><td>Recycled steel</td><td>15,000</td><td>1,200</td><td>750</td></tr><tr><td>Low-carbon concrete bricks</td><td>10,000</td><td>200</td><td>175</td></tr><tr><td>Hempcrete</td><td>7,500</td><td>40</td><td>600</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2"><b>Total</b></td><td><b>33,800 t CO₂</b></td><td><b>$28.3M</b></td></tr></table></div>`},
     num:{value:25.5e6, tol:.02, unit:"$ cost keeping mineral wool"},
     listen:["Cap: 110% × $23.6M ≈ $26M; low-carbon costs $28.3M, so cut ~$2.3M+","Shortcut: concrete and steel drive emissions with <10% cost premium: keep","Hempcrete costs 2.7× mineral wool: drop it","Cost delta (600 − 225) × 7,500 ≈ $2.8M → ~$25.5M","Emissions delta (70 − 40) × 7,500 = 225 t: <1%","Emissions still ~50% lower"],
     ifwrong:[["Drops recycled steel","\"Steel is a big emitter with a small premium. Which material has the biggest premium for the least impact?\""]],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">Cap = 110% × $23.6M ≈ $26.0M (low-carbon = $28.3M)\nHempcrete vs. mineral wool cost: (600 − 225) × 7,500 ≈ $2.8M → $28.3M − $2.8M ≈ $25.5M ✓\nEmissions: (70 − 40) kg × 7,500 t = 225 t CO₂ → 225 ÷ 72,275 &lt; 1%\nNew emissions ≈ 34,025 t: still ~50% below 72,275</p><p>Concrete and steel deliver most of the reduction for a small cost premium; hempcrete adds a lot of cost for almost no emissions benefit.</p>",
     stuck:["\"Which material has the biggest cost premium per ton?\"","\"What's 110% of the baseline cost?\""]},
    {name:"Resident initiatives", time:"3 min",
     say:["For the RFP, the client also wants initiatives that enable and encourage future residents to reduce their emissions. What ideas can you suggest?"],
     listen:["Groups ideas: mobility, energy, awareness, circular economy"],
     check:["Public transport with local authorities","Walkable, shaded streets and cycle lanes","EV charging and incentives","Renewable energy subscriptions","Smart energy tracking","Urban gardens","Gamified energy-saving app","Sustainability campaigns and challenges","Easy access to carbon offsets","Shared tools and appliances","Home waste sorting","Community composting","Recycling rewards"],
     key:"<p><b>Mobility</b>: public transport, walkable shaded streets and cycle lanes, EV charging and perks. <b>Energy</b>: renewable subscriptions, smart tracking, urban gardens. <b>Awareness</b>: gamified app, campaigns, collective challenges, carbon offsets. <b>Circular economy</b>: shared tools, waste sorting, composting, recycling rewards.</p>"},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["What is your final recommendation to your client?"],
     listen:["Restates the objective: minimize GHG, stay cost-competitive","Material plan: low-carbon concrete, steel, bricks; keep mineral wool → ~50% less, within 110%","Add resident initiatives","Next: assess the most impactful initiatives"],
     key:"<p>\"You asked how to show minimal emissions at a competitive cost. Design and construction are already optimized, so strengthen the bid with materials and resident initiatives. Switch concrete, steel and bricks to low-carbon versions but keep mineral wool: that halves material emissions while keeping cost within 110% of conventional materials. Also propose initiatives that help residents cut emissions: sustainable mobility, clean energy and energy-saving programs. Next, assess which initiatives have the most impact on emissions.\"</p>"}
  ]
},
{
  id:"deptstore", title:"Department Store Co", type:"Strategy (segmentation)", diff:"Medium", mins:30,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["Strategy"], sectors:["Retail"], stretch:["Structuring","Judgement & insights"], video:false, rating:null, downloads:null},
  plain:"A US department store chain is losing market share. The case opens unusually, with a brainstorm on customer segmentation. The candidate then structures growth strategies, compares two marketing campaigns' profit (Trendy vs. Bargain shoppers), and proposes ways to raise Trendy shoppers' spend.",
  bottom:"Target the <b>Trendy</b> segment: +10pp penetration = 24M × 10% × $240 = <b>$576M</b> profit, vs. Bargain +15pp = 48M × 15% × $60 = <b>$432M</b>. Higher spend and margin beat the bigger penetration gain. Then raise Trendy spend through assortment, digital engagement and incentives.",
  prompt:["Department Store Co. is a leading US national department store, selling apparel, footwear, home goods and kitchen supplies, online and in physical stores.","Despite its historic leadership, it faces a declining department store sector: less foot traffic, shrinking profitability, and more competition from e-commerce and digitally native brands.","It has recently lost market share. The team has been asked to identify the key customer segments in the market and choose which ones to focus on to improve profitability."],
  clarify:[
    ["What's the time frame?","Strategies that can be implemented within the next three years."],
    ["Can they enter new businesses or countries?","No. The goal is to increase market share and profitability of the existing business, not new opportunities or geographies."],
    ["Online and offline?","Both: it operates stores and a website."]
  ],
  openListen:["Plays back: lost market share; find and choose customer segments","Asks about time frame (3 years)","Asks about scope (existing business only)"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Segment brainstorm", time:"4 min",
     say:["Let's start with a brainstorm. Department Store Co. wants to understand the different groups, or 'segments', of customers that shop at its stores and website. What attributes would you consider when thinking about groups of people it might target?"],
     steer:["Original case: after the attributes, ask the candidate to propose 3 or 4 customer segments.","Explain that a segment is usually a mix of demographic, behavioral and attitudinal attributes, e.g. 'female shoppers over 60'.","Steer them if needed toward both qualitative and quantitative attributes. There's no single correct answer."],
     listen:["Groups attributes: demographic, attitudinal, behavioral","Then proposes 3–4 named segments"],
     check:["Demographic: age, gender","Demographic: household income","Demographic: life stage (student, professional, parent)","Attitude: fashion interest (trendy, classic, disengaged)","Attitude: price sensitivity vs. brand loyalty","Attitude: quality vs. affordability","Attitude: convenience, sustainability, exclusivity","Behavior: annual spend at department stores","Behavior: breadth of retailers shopped","Behavior: channel (in-store, online, omnichannel)","Behavior: frequency and returns"],
     probe:["\"Now name 3 or 4 segments using those attributes.\""],
     key:"<p><b>Demographic</b> (age, gender, income, life stage), <b>attitudinal</b> (fashion interest, price sensitivity vs. loyalty, quality vs. affordability, convenience, sustainability, exclusivity), <b>behavioral</b> (annual spend, breadth of retailers, channel, frequency and returns). Example segments: <b>Trendy Student</b> (&lt;30, frequent, trend-led, price-conscious, omnichannel); <b>Classic Parent</b> (45+, household shopper, quality over trend, in-store); <b>Value Hunter</b> (all ages, price-sensitive, low loyalty, many channels).</p>"},
    {name:"Structure: growth strategies", time:"3 min",
     say:["What are some strategies Department Store Co. could pursue to increase its market share and address its challenges?"],
     steer:["Original case: if the candidate starts brainstorming, guide them to organize their ideas after the first few."],
     listen:["Structures: more customers × more visits × more spend per visit","Concrete ideas in each"],
     check:["Customers: expand digital (e-commerce, mobile, pop-ups)","Customers: target younger segments (influencers, social)","Customers: new categories (beauty, wellness)","Customers: tailored messaging per shopper type","Frequency: loyalty program","Frequency: win back lapsed customers","Frequency: omnichannel (click-and-collect, easy returns)","Spend: bundles and lifestyle collections","Spend: personalized promotions","Spend: broader assortment (accessories, home)"],
     key:"<p><b>1. More customers</b>: digital channels and pop-ups, younger segments via influencers and social, new categories, tailored messaging. <b>2. More frequent visits</b>: loyalty program, win back lapsed customers, better omnichannel. <b>3. Higher spend per visit</b>: bundles and collections, personalized promotions, wider assortment.</p>"},
    {name:"Trendy vs. Bargain", time:"6 min",
     say:["Given time and resources, the client wants to focus on increasing share among lower-penetration segments. Internal research identified three segments that together make up the market. The marketing team believes a campaign could achieve either +10pp penetration among Trendy shoppers (30% → 40%) or +15pp among Bargain shoppers (30% → 45%). With equal marketing costs, and only penetration changing, which option generates more additional profit?"],
     give:{when:"Right away (Exhibits 1 and 2).",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 1.</b> Overview of customer segments</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Segment</th><th>Age group</th><th>Fashion focus</th><th>Price sensitivity</th></tr><tr><td>Bargain</td><td>All ages</td><td>Low: durability and basics over style</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Classic</td><td>45+</td><td>Medium: timeless, high-quality clothing</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>Trendy</td><td>&lt;30</td><td>High: seasonal, fashion-forward looks</td><td>Moderate</td></tr></table></div><p><b>Exhibit 2.</b> Segment share and profitability</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Segment</th><th>% of US households</th><th>Avg. spend per year at dept. stores</th><th>% of spend at Dept. Store Co.</th><th>Avg. COGS per customer</th></tr><tr><td>Bargain</td><td>40%</td><td>$200</td><td>30%</td><td>$140</td></tr><tr><td>Classic</td><td>40%</td><td>$1,000</td><td>60%</td><td>$650</td></tr><tr><td>Trendy</td><td>20%</td><td>$600</td><td>30%</td><td>$360</td></tr></table></div><p class="muted">Assume 120M households, one shopper each; fixed costs don't change.</p>`},
     num:{value:576e6, tol:.03, unit:"$ extra profit (best option)"},
     listen:["Formula: households × segment share × penetration gain × profit per customer","Bargain: 48M × 15% × ($200 − $140) = $432M","Trendy: 24M × 10% × ($600 − $360) = $576M","Choose Trendy: higher spend and margin beat the larger gain","Next: test whether +10pp is feasible; pilot"],
     ifwrong:[["Uses spend instead of profit","\"Which number tells you what we keep from each customer?\""]],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">Bargain: 120M × 40% = 48M × 15% × ($200 − $140 = $60) = $432M\nTrendy:  120M × 20% = 24M × 10% × ($600 − $360 = $240) = $576M</p><p>Prioritize <b>Trendy</b>: a smaller penetration gain, but 4× the profit per customer. Next: validate the 10pp assumption with a pilot and track segment performance.</p>",
     stuck:["\"How many Trendy shoppers are there?\"","\"How much profit does one Trendy shopper bring?\""]},
    {name:"Raising Trendy spend", time:"3 min",
     say:["We've looked at increasing Trendy penetration. The client now wants a second lever: increasing average spend per customer. What specific strategies could raise the annual spend of Trendy shoppers?"],
     listen:["Three levers: product assortment, digital engagement, purchase incentives","Ideas fit young, trend-led shoppers"],
     check:["Limited-edition capsule collections and drops","Adjacent high-margin categories (accessories, beauty, wellness)","'Shop the look' bundles","Personalized recommendations and promotions","AI styling and virtual try-on","User-generated content and influencer proof","Tiered points-based loyalty","Time-limited spend offers","CRM to re-engage lapsed Trendy shoppers"],
     key:"<p><b>Assortment</b>: capsule drops, high-margin adjacencies (accessories, beauty, wellness), 'shop the look' bundles. <b>Digital engagement</b>: personalized recommendations, AI styling and virtual try-on, social proof. <b>Purchase incentives</b>: tiered loyalty, time-limited spend offers, CRM win-back.</p>"},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["You run into the CEO in the cafeteria at the client site. She asks what you think about Department Store Co.'s ability to increase market share and profitability. What do you tell her?"],
     listen:["Restates the question","Answer: clear opportunity in the under-penetrated, high-margin Trendy segment","Strong in Classic; pressure from digital players among the young","Trendy: 20% of market, we capture 30% of spend; +10pp ≈ $576M","No silver bullet: marketing, fast-moving product, digital","Next: pilot and scale; digital and personalization; longer-term levers; adapt to other segments"],
     key:"<p>\"There's a clear opportunity: the under-penetrated but high-margin Trendy segment. We're strong with Classic shoppers, but digital-first players are pressuring us with younger customers. Trendy is 20% of households and we capture only 30% of their spend; raising that to 40% would add about $576M in profit. There's no silver bullet, but tailored marketing, fast-moving products and a better digital experience can get us there. Next: pilot and scale Trendy initiatives, invest in personalization, look at longer-term levers like partnerships or new formats, and see which ideas could grow share of wallet in our other segments.\"</p>"}
  ]
},
{
  id:"inflightwifi", title:"In-flight Wi-Fi", type:"Market entry", diff:"Medium", mins:30,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["Market entry"], sectors:["Technology","Airlines","Telecom"], stretch:["Judgement & insights"], video:false, rating:null, downloads:null},
  plain:"The leading supplier of cockpit communication equipment wants to sell in-flight Wi-Fi equipment, both for new planes and to retrofit existing ones. The candidate sizes the market from fleet and production charts, judges how fast retrofits will be adopted (an S-curve), and works out how to convince a reluctant low-cost airline.",
  bottom:"New aircraft: 1,200 × $1.5M = <b>$1.8B a year</b>. Retrofit: 70% of 38,000 = 26,600 planes × $1.5M ≈ <b>$40B one-off</b>; at +5pp adoption a year ≈ <b>$2.85B</b> in Year N. Total ≈ <b>$4.65B</b> (~70% of SkyCo's $7B sales). Recommend preparing entry, with a stage-gated investment.",
  prompt:["Your client, SkyCo, is the leading telecommunications equipment supplier to aviation. Its hardware and software let pilots talk to air traffic controllers, and it works with major aircraft makers like Airbus and Boeing.","SkyCo wants to accelerate revenue growth and is considering entering the in-flight Wi-Fi equipment market. It would supply hardware and software to aircraft makers for new aircraft, and to airlines to retrofit existing aircraft without Wi-Fi.","The equipment is a one-time installation that lasts the aircraft's lifetime, with no ongoing maintenance. Airlines control the passenger Wi-Fi service and may earn money from it; that revenue is out of scope.","SkyCo has asked for your support in evaluating this opportunity."],
  clarify:[
    ["How big is SkyCo?","The leading player in aviation telecom equipment, with ~$7B in sales last year."],
    ["What's the objective?","Significant revenue growth without hurting overall profitability. No specific revenue target."],
    ["Can they build it?","It would need end-to-end development, but R&D is confident: the technology is simpler than SkyCo's current products."],
    ["Which geography?","It's a global opportunity."]
  ],
  openListen:["Plays back: two customer groups (new aircraft, retrofits), one-time sale","Asks about objective and SkyCo's size","Asks about technical capability"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"3 min",
     say:["How should we evaluate this opportunity?"],
     listen:["Market attractiveness: size (new vs. retrofit) and growth","SkyCo's ability to win: customers' needs, competitors, differentiation","Profitability: investment, revenue, running costs","Capabilities and risks"],
     check:["Market: size (new aircraft, retrofits)","Market: growth and its drivers","Customers: manufacturers (cost, reliability, weight, space)","Customers: airlines (same, plus fast installation)","Competitors vs. purchase criteria","SkyCo's edge","Profit: initial investment (R&D, factories)","Profit: revenue","Profit: fixed and variable running costs","Capabilities: Wi-Fi and satellite R&D for aviation, low-cost manufacturing","Risks: R&D overrun, disruption, competitive response, air-travel shocks"],
     probe:["\"What do airlines care about that manufacturers don't?\""],
     key:"<p><b>1. Is the market attractive?</b> Size (new aircraft, retrofits) and growth (drivers, sustainability). <b>2. Can SkyCo win?</b> Customers and their criteria (manufacturers: cost, reliability, weight, space; airlines: also fast installation), competitors, SkyCo's edge. <b>3. Is it profitable?</b> Investment, revenue, running costs. <b>4. Capabilities and risks</b>: aviation-grade Wi-Fi R&D and manufacturing; R&D overruns, disruption, competitor response, air-travel shocks.</p>"},
    {name:"Market size", time:"6 min",
     say:["Let's estimate the revenue potential. Based on Exhibits 1 and 2, can you estimate the potential market size (in $) for in-flight Wi-Fi equipment globally, in Year N?"],
     steer:["Original case: the candidate should size both new aircraft and retrofits.","For the annual retrofit market they need a yearly uptake. If they need a hint, suggest using the speed of adoption. Slightly different answers are fine with different assumptions."],
     give:{when:"Exhibits right away; the price if asked.",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 1.</b> Commercial passenger aircraft: worldwide fleet and in-flight Wi-Fi adoption</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Year</th><th>N-9</th><th>N-8</th><th>N-7</th><th>N-6</th><th>N-5</th><th>N-4</th><th>N-3</th><th>N-2</th><th>N-1</th><th>N (today)</th></tr><tr><td>Fleet (aircraft)</td><td>32,300</td><td>32,900</td><td>33,500</td><td>34,100</td><td>34,500</td><td>35,000</td><td>35,800</td><td>36,500</td><td>37,300</td><td>38,000</td></tr><tr><td>Wi-Fi adoption</td><td>5%</td><td>6%</td><td>7%</td><td>9%</td><td>11%</td><td>14%</td><td>17%</td><td>21%</td><td>25%</td><td>30%</td></tr></table></div><p><b>Exhibit 2.</b> Annual production of new commercial passenger aircraft</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Year</th><th>N-9</th><th>N-8</th><th>N-7</th><th>N-6</th><th>N-5</th><th>N-4</th><th>N-3</th><th>N-2</th><th>N-1</th><th>N</th></tr><tr><td>New aircraft</td><td>740</td><td>800</td><td>860</td><td>920</td><td>1,000</td><td>1,050</td><td>1,070</td><td>1,100</td><td>1,120</td><td>1,200</td></tr></table></div><p class="muted">If asked: price per unit today (Year N) is <b>$1.5M</b>.</p>`},
     num:{min:4.2e9, max:5.1e9, unit:"$ market in Year N"},
     listen:["Segments: new aircraft vs. retrofit","New: 1,200 × $1.5M = $1.8B a year (assumes 100% equipped)","Retrofit stock: 70% × 38,000 = 26,600 × $1.5M ≈ $40B one-off","Year N retrofits: adoption +5pp → 38,000 × 5% × $1.5M = $2.85B","Total ≈ $4.65B, ~70% of SkyCo's sales","Caveats: not every plane needs Wi-Fi; price may fall; retrofit is one-off; production growing"],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">New aircraft: 1,200 × $1.5M = $1.8B a year\nRetrofit pool: 70% × 38,000 = 26,600 × $1.5M ≈ $39.9B (one-off, over years)\nYear N retrofits: 38,000 × 5% (adoption +5pp) × $1.5M = $2.85B\nYear N total ≈ $4.65B (vs. SkyCo's ~$7B)</p><p>Caveats: assumes 100% adoption (low-cost or short-haul planes may skip it) and a stable price (it may fall with competition). New-aircraft demand grows with production; the retrofit market is one-off and depends on adoption speed.</p>",
     stuck:["\"What are the two sources of demand?\"","\"How many planes got Wi-Fi last year? Look at the adoption line.\""]},
    {name:"Retrofit adoption speed", time:"4 min",
     say:["Let's dive into the retrofit opportunity: equipping existing aircraft. How fast do you think this market will be captured, and what factors, positive and negative, would influence the speed of adoption?"],
     listen:["S-curve: slow, then fast, then saturation","Accelerators and barriers","Uses the data: +1pp (N-8), +3pp (N-3), +5pp (N): still accelerating","Estimate: retrofit unfolds over ~10–15 years"],
     check:["Accelerator: passengers expect connectivity","Accelerator: airline competition and differentiation","Accelerator: ancillary revenue","Accelerator: partnerships, bundled retrofits","Accelerator: falling costs","Barrier: $1.5M upfront capex","Barrier: grounding during installation","Barrier: logistics across varied fleets","Barrier: downturns, fuel shocks","Barrier: uneven regulation"],
     probe:["\"What does the adoption trend tell you about where we are on the curve?\""],
     key:"<p>Expect an <b>S-curve</b>. <b>Accelerators</b>: passenger expectations, airline competition, ancillary revenue, partnerships with makers or lessors, falling costs. <b>Barriers</b>: $1.5M upfront per plane, grounding time, logistics across mixed fleets, economic shocks, uneven regulation. Adoption grew +1pp in N-8, +3pp in N-3 and +5pp in N: <b>still accelerating</b>. A realistic estimate: the retrofit market unfolds over <b>10–15 years</b>, accelerating for a few more years before slowing.</p>"},
    {name:"Convincing a low-cost airline", time:"3 min",
     say:["Imagine a low-cost airline's CEO is reluctant to invest in Wi-Fi equipment. How could SkyCo convince them?"],
     listen:["Reframe Wi-Fi as a strategic asset, not a cost","Address the upfront cost and downtime concerns"],
     check:["Flexible pricing: revenue-sharing or leasing","Ancillary revenue: paid access, ads, e-commerce","Operational gains: real-time flight data, crew tools","Customer experience and competitiveness","Quick-install, lightweight equipment"],
     key:"<p>Reframe Wi-Fi as a <b>strategic asset</b>: flexible pricing (revenue-sharing, leasing) to avoid upfront cost; ancillary revenue from paid access, ads and e-commerce; operational gains (real-time data, crew tools); better customer experience; fast-install, lightweight kits to cut downtime.</p>"},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["What is your recommendation to your client at this stage, and what are the next steps?"],
     listen:["Answer first: investigate further and prepare to enter","~$4.65B a year (~70% of current revenue); ~$40B retrofit pool, adoption accelerating","Relationships with manufacturers; technology within capability","Caveats: upfront investment, high unit price, retrofit is one-off","Next: business case, competitors, customer interviews, operating model, stage-gated plan"],
     key:"<p>\"You asked us to evaluate in-flight Wi-Fi. I'd recommend preparing to enter: the market is about $4.65B a year, nearly 70% of SkyCo's revenue, with a ~$40B retrofit pool and accelerating adoption. SkyCo already has relationships with aircraft makers and the technology is within its R&D capabilities. Caveats: significant upfront investment, a high $1.5M price that requires convincing customers, and a retrofit market that eventually ends, so plan recurring revenue early. Next: a detailed business case, a competitor map, customer interviews, an operating model, and a stage-gated investment plan.\"</p>"}
  ]
}
);
Object.assign(window.CASE_ADDITIONS,{
  patati:{},
  lowcarbon:{},
  deptstore:{clarify:[2]},
  inflightwifi:{}
});
Object.assign(window.CASE_SCRIPTS,{
patati:{
 open:"<p><b>To recap</b>: Patati launched potato wedges in the UK, US and Japan three years ago. The US and Japan are doing well, but UK profits are falling. <b>The CEO wants to turn the UK around.</b> Could I ask whether the decline is only in the UK, what success looks like, and how many wedge products we sell there?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>Profit is falling, so I'd check revenue and costs.</b> <b>Revenue</b>: are we selling fewer wedges, either because the UK market is shrinking or because we're losing customers to rivals, and has our price fallen? <b>Costs</b>: have they risen anywhere along the chain: raw materials, production, distribution, or sales and marketing? <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Revenue by market":"<p><b>UK revenue fell 25%</b>, from about 8 to 6 million. But <b>the launch was a success</b>: in Year 1 the UK sold almost as much as the US and Japan, probably much bigger markets. Since then the other two have grown fast while the UK has declined, <b>so something changed after launch</b>, maybe competitors discounting.</p>",
  "Why volume fell":"<p><b>If price didn't change, we're selling fewer units.</b> I'd look at five causes. <b>Customers</b> moving away from wedges, or toward a type we don't sell, like low-fat. <b>Competitors</b> with better or cheaper products. <b>Distribution</b>: lost retailers or shelf space. <b>Our product</b>: quality issues or bad reviews. <b>Marketing</b>: too little spend after launch, or the wrong channels.</p>",
  "How competitors cut price":"<p><b>Cutting a third of the price without losing profit means competitors cut costs</b>, probably in the biggest cost. Could I ask what our cost breakdown looks like? <i>[75% is potatoes, transport and import duties.]</i> Then the gap is almost certainly in <b>potato sourcing</b>. If competitors grow the variety in UK greenhouses, they avoid import duties and transport. <b>So our imported potatoes are the key cost disadvantage.</b></p>",
  "UK potato investment":"<p><b>I'll compare investing with doing nothing.</b> Today we sell about 1 million portions at $6. <b>If we invest</b>, price goes 6, 5, then 4, and units 1, 1.5, then 2 million: revenue of about <b>$37.5 million</b> over five years, so <b>$7.5 million profit</b>. <b>If we don't</b>, we lose 15% a year: about $22 million, so <b>$4.5 million profit</b>. Net of the $3 million investment, <b>it roughly breaks even in this window, and wins clearly after that</b>, because the alternative is to keep declining until we exit.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>Patati should invest in growing potatoes in the UK.</b> Three reasons: competitors already do it successfully and have a cost advantage; our imported potatoes force a higher price that drives customers away; and investing beats continued decline. <b>Next</b>, I'd identify the farming skills we'd need and plan how to get them, and look at short-term ways to win customers back.</p>"
 }},
lowcarbon:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me make sure I understand.</b> Our client is bidding to build a landmark low-emission residential district in the Middle East. <b>To win, it must show minimal greenhouse gas emissions at a competitive cost.</b> Could I ask which emissions count, whether there's a target, and whether the client builds in the region itself?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd follow the project's life.</b> <b>During construction</b>: lower-carbon materials, local sourcing and cleaner transport, and cleaner equipment and on-site energy. <b>During use</b>: shared district cooling, energy-efficient design with passive cooling, good orientation and insulation, on-site solar, EV charging and public transport, and possibly carbon capture. <b>Throughout, we'd check every lever against cost, since the bid must stay competitive.</b> <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Material criteria":"<p><b>I'd use five groups of criteria.</b> <b>Environmental</b>: emissions from production to installation, renewability, local availability. <b>Economic</b>: upfront and lifetime cost, and whether it scales. <b>Practical</b>: performance, durability, ease of construction. <b>Market and regulatory</b>: enough suppliers, building standards, incentives. <b>Social</b>: how it looks and feels for residents.</p>",
  "Materials within budget":"<p><b>The cap is 110% of $23.6 million, about $26 million</b>, and the low-carbon version costs $28.3 million. <b>Shortcut</b>: concrete and steel drive most emissions and cost under 10% more, so keep them. <b>Hempcrete costs nearly three times mineral wool.</b> Keeping mineral wool saves 375 dollars a ton on 7,500 tons, <b>about $2.8 million</b>, bringing us to <b>$25.5 million</b>, within budget. It adds just 225 tons of CO₂, <b>under 1%</b>, so <b>we still cut emissions by about half</b>.</p>",
  "Resident initiatives":"<p><b>Four areas.</b> <b>Mobility</b>: public transport, shaded walkable streets, cycle lanes and EV charging. <b>Energy</b>: renewable tariffs and smart meters. <b>Awareness</b>: a gamified energy app and community challenges. <b>Circular economy</b>: shared tools, home waste sorting, composting and recycling rewards.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>You asked how to show minimal emissions at a competitive cost.</b> Switch concrete, steel and bricks to low-carbon versions but <b>keep mineral wool</b>: that <b>halves material emissions within 110% of the conventional cost</b>. Add a package of resident initiatives for mobility, clean energy and saving energy. <b>Next</b>, rank those initiatives by their emissions impact.</p>"
 }},
deptstore:{
 open:"<p><b>To recap</b>: Department Store Co. is a US leader in a declining sector, losing share to e-commerce and digital brands. <b>We need to find the key customer segments and pick which ones to focus on to improve profitability.</b> Could I ask what time frame we have, and whether new businesses or countries are in scope?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Segment brainstorm":"<p><b>I'd group attributes into three types.</b> <b>Who they are</b>: age, gender, income, life stage. <b>What they value</b>: fashion interest, price versus brand loyalty, quality versus affordability, convenience. <b>How they shop</b>: annual spend, how many retailers they use, online or in-store, how often they buy and return. <b>Combining them, I'd see three segments</b>: the Trendy Student, young and trend-led; the Classic Parent, 45-plus, quality-first and in-store; and the Value Hunter, price-driven at any age.</p>",
  "Structure: growth strategies":"<p><b>Sales are customers times visits times spend per visit, so I'd grow each.</b> <b>More customers</b>: stronger digital, influencers to reach younger shoppers, new categories like beauty. <b>More visits</b>: a loyalty program, win-back offers, and easy click-and-collect and returns. <b>More spend</b>: bundles and curated collections, personalized promotions, and a broader assortment.</p>",
  "Trendy vs. Bargain":"<p><b>Extra profit is shoppers times penetration gain times profit per shopper.</b> <b>Bargain</b>: 48 million households, plus 15 points, times $60 profit each, is <b>$432 million</b>. <b>Trendy</b>: 24 million, plus 10 points, times $240, is <b>$576 million</b>. <b>So Trendy wins</b>: a smaller gain, but each shopper is worth four times as much. I'd validate the 10-point assumption with a pilot.</p>",
  "Raising Trendy spend":"<p><b>Three levers.</b> <b>Assortment</b>: limited-edition drops, accessories and beauty, and 'shop the look' bundles. <b>Digital</b>: personalized recommendations, virtual try-on, and influencer and customer content. <b>Incentives</b>: tiered loyalty points, time-limited spend offers, and win-back offers for lapsed shoppers.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>There's a clear opportunity in the Trendy segment.</b> We're strong with Classic shoppers, but digital players are taking young customers. Trendy is 20% of households and we capture only 30% of their spend; <b>reaching 40% adds about $576 million of profit</b>. No silver bullet, but tailored marketing, fast-moving products and a better digital experience will get us there. <b>Next</b>: pilot these ideas, invest in personalization, and test which could work for our other segments.</p>"
 }},
inflightwifi:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me make sure I understand.</b> SkyCo leads in cockpit communication equipment and wants to grow by selling in-flight Wi-Fi equipment, to manufacturers for new planes and to airlines for retrofits. It's a one-time sale per plane. <b>The question is whether to enter.</b> Could I ask how big SkyCo is, what the objective is, and whether it has the technical capability?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd ask four questions.</b> <b>Is the market attractive?</b> Its size, from new planes and retrofits, and its growth. <b>Can SkyCo win?</b> What manufacturers and airlines each need, such as cost, weight, reliability and fast installation, and how SkyCo compares to competitors. <b>Is it profitable?</b> Investment, revenue and running costs. <b>What capabilities and risks</b> are involved? <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Market size":"<p><b>Two sources of demand.</b> <b>New planes</b>: 1,200 a year at $1.5M each is <b>$1.8 billion a year</b>, if all are equipped. <b>Retrofits</b>: 70% of the 38,000-plane fleet has no Wi-Fi, so 26,600 planes, <b>about $40 billion</b> in total over many years. Adoption rose 5 points last year, so this year that's 1,900 planes, <b>$2.85 billion</b>. <b>Total: about $4.65 billion this year</b>, close to 70% of SkyCo's sales. <b>So it's large</b>, but retrofits are one-off and prices may fall.</p>",
  "Retrofit adoption speed":"<p><b>I'd expect an S-curve</b>: slow, then fast, then flattening. <b>Accelerators</b>: passengers expect Wi-Fi, airlines compete on it, it can earn money, and costs fall. <b>Barriers</b>: $1.5M per plane, grounding time, mixed fleets and economic shocks. <b>The data says we're accelerating</b>: adoption grew 1 point a year early on, 3 points mid-period and 5 last year. <b>So I'd expect the retrofit market to play out over 10 to 15 years.</b></p>",
  "Convincing a low-cost airline":"<p><b>I'd sell Wi-Fi as an investment, not a cost.</b> Remove the upfront hit with <b>leasing or revenue-sharing</b>. Show the <b>new revenue</b> from paid access, ads and onboard shopping. Add <b>operational benefits</b> like real-time data for crews. And offer a <b>light, quick-install kit</b> so planes aren't grounded for long.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I'd recommend SkyCo prepares to enter.</b> The market is about $4.65 billion a year, nearly 70% of its revenue, with a $40 billion retrofit pool and accelerating adoption. It already knows the manufacturers, and the technology is within reach. <b>Caveats</b>: big upfront investment, a high price to justify, and retrofits that eventually run out. <b>Next</b>: a full business case, a competitor map, customer interviews, and a stage-gated investment plan.</p>"
 }}
});
