window.CASES.push(
{
  id:"viraloutbreak", title:"Viral Outbreak", type:"Public sector strategy", diff:"Medium", mins:30,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["Strategy"], sectors:["Public sector"], stretch:["Structuring"], video:false, rating:4.6, downloads:15605},
  plain:"A country's health commission wants to prevent an outbreak of a new airborne virus. The candidate structures prevention, diagnosis and treatment, reads flu-season charts carefully (what the data does and does not prove), compares a pill vaccine with an injection using expected values, and designs border measures.",
  bottom:"Country A has the worst flu infection curve (first past 15 per 100k); learn from Countries B and C. The hospitalization chart can't show who is most at risk without more data. <b>Adopt the pill</b>: injection 60%×0.01% + 40%×10% = 4.006% vs. pill 90%×0.83% + 10%×10% = 1.747% → <b>~11M fewer infections</b> across 500M people. Add border measures.",
  prompt:["The World Health Organization has identified a new strain of airborne virus and warned it could lead to an epidemic if countries aren't prepared.","Country A's Health Commission is concerned. The country has had several damaging epidemics and wants to avoid another outbreak, so it has asked your team for support.","How can the Health Commission prevent a viral outbreak in Country A?"],
  clarify:[
    ["What counts as an outbreak?","More than 15 infections per 100,000 people for two consecutive weeks."],
    ["How big is the country?","500 million people."],
    ["Is there a vaccine?","We'll come to vaccines shortly."]
  ],
  openListen:["Asks how an outbreak is defined (15 per 100k for 2 weeks)","Plays back: prevent, not treat, an outbreak"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"3 min",
     say:["How would you structure your approach to this problem?"],
     listen:["Prevention: awareness, vaccine, tools, measures to slow spread","Diagnosis: test available, accessible, capacity, speed","Treatment: effective, accessible, capacity"],
     check:["Prevention: awareness of prevention and symptoms","Prevention: vaccine available, affordable, accessible","Prevention: other tools (masks)","Prevention: quarantines, travel restrictions","Diagnosis: reliable test","Diagnosis: accessible test","Diagnosis: testing capacity","Diagnosis: fast results","Treatment: effective treatment","Treatment: accessible","Treatment: capacity to treat"],
     probe:["\"Once someone is infected, how do you stop them infecting others?\""],
     key:"<p><b>1. Prevention</b>: awareness of prevention and symptoms; vaccine available, affordable, accessible; other tools like masks; measures to slow transmission. <b>2. Diagnosis</b>: a reliable, accessible test with enough capacity and fast results. <b>3. Treatment</b>: an effective, accessible treatment and enough capacity.</p>"},
    {name:"Flu season data", time:"5 min",
     say:["The client shared two exhibits on infection rates and hospitalizations in a typical flu season. The Health Commission thinks flu is a reasonable proxy for how the new virus could spread. What can you conclude?"],
     steer:["Original case: the key judgement is on Exhibit 2. It shows the mix of hospitalized patients, not hospitalization rates, so it can't prove older people or those with pre-existing conditions are more at risk."],
     give:{when:"Right away (Exhibits 1 and 2).",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 1.</b> Infection rates in a typical flu season (infections per 100,000 people, read from a bar chart)</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Week</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>6</th><th>7</th><th>8</th><th>9</th></tr><tr><td>Country A</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>8</td><td>14</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Country B</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1.5</td><td>2.5</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>4.5</td><td>6</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Country C</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>2.5</td><td>4</td><td>4.5</td><td>5</td><td>5.5</td><td>7</td><td>7.5</td></tr><tr><td>Country D</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>4.5</td><td>6</td><td>10</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>Country E</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0.5</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>5.5</td></tr></table></div><p><b>Exhibit 2.</b> Hospitalized flu patients by age, with and without a pre-existing condition¹ (share of hospitalized patients)</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Age</th><th>&lt;5</th><th>5–15</th><th>16–44</th><th>45–64</th><th>65+</th></tr><tr><td>With</td><td>19%</td><td>42%</td><td>48%</td><td>69%</td><td>76%</td></tr><tr><td>Without</td><td>81%</td><td>58%</td><td>52%</td><td>31%</td><td>24%</td></tr></table></div><p class="muted">¹ Any condition that weakens or compromises a person's immune system.</p>`},
     listen:["Country A has among the highest rates and is first past 15 per 100k","Gap with neighbors widening: A's prevention looks weaker","B and C keep infections low: benchmarks","Exhibit 2: older patients more often have pre-existing conditions (19% → 76%)","But it can't show older people or those with conditions are more likely hospitalized: need rates by age and condition prevalence","Next: study B and C's measures; get hospitalization rates"],
     ifwrong:[["Says older people are more likely to be hospitalized","\"Does this chart tell you how many people of each age are hospitalized?\""]],
     key:"<p><b>Exhibit 1</b>: a widening gap between Country A and its neighbors. A is first past the 15 per 100k threshold, so its prevention looks weaker; B and C limit infections well and could be benchmarks. <b>Exhibit 2</b>: among hospitalized patients, the share with pre-existing conditions rises with age (19% → 76%). But it <b>doesn't show</b> that older people are more likely to be hospitalized (no rates by age), or that conditions raise the risk (no prevalence data in the general population). Next: learn B and C's measures; get hospitalization rates and condition prevalence by age.</p>",
     stuck:["\"Which country crosses the outbreak threshold first?\"","\"What would you need to know to say a group is at higher risk?\""]},
    {name:"Pill vs. injection", time:"5 min",
     say:["A major pharmaceutical company has developed a vaccine that can be taken as a pill instead of an injection. The Commission believes the pill would be easier to distribute and could reach 90% of the population, vs. 60% for the injection. But it's slightly less effective: the probability of infection for vaccinated people is 0.83% with the pill vs. 0.01% with the injection. Without vaccination it's 10%. Should the Health Commission adopt the pill?"],
     steer:["Original case: the candidate should compare expected infection rates for injection vs. pill and quantify the impact on total infections."],
     give:{when:"If asked.", html:"<p>Country A has a population of <b>500 million</b>.</p>"},
     num:{value:11.3e6, tol:.05, unit:"fewer infections with the pill"},
     listen:["Expected rate = % vaccinated × vaccinated rate + % unvaccinated × 10%","Injection: 60%×0.01% + 40%×10% = 4.006%","Pill: 90%×0.83% + 10%×10% = 1.747%","Difference ≈ 2.26% × 500M ≈ 11M fewer infections","Adopt the pill: coverage beats per-person efficacy","Next: affordability, distribution at scale"],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">Injection: 60% × 0.01% + 40% × 10% = 0.006% + 4% = 4.006%\nPill:      90% × 0.83% + 10% × 10% = 0.747% + 1% = 1.747%\nDifference ≈ 2.26% × 500M ≈ 11M fewer infections</p><p>Adopt the pill: it's less effective per person, but much higher coverage more than offsets that. Next: confirm affordability and distribution at scale.</p>",
     stuck:["\"What's the expected infection rate across the whole population with each option?\""]},
    {name:"Imported cases", time:"3 min",
     say:["So far we've focused on transmission within Country A. The Director is encouraged, but worried about infected travelers bringing the virus in. What measures could reduce this risk?"],
     listen:["Three layers: stop entry, detect early, limit onward spread"],
     check:["Entry requirements (vaccination, testing)","Travel restrictions for high-risk regions","Health declarations, symptom screening","Monitoring at airports, ports, borders","Testing high-risk travelers","Contact tracing and surveillance","Quarantine or isolation","Public awareness on symptoms","Rapid-response protocols"],
     key:"<p><b>Stop infected people entering</b>: entry requirements, restrictions on high-risk regions, declarations and screening. <b>Detect imported cases early</b>: border monitoring, testing high-risk travelers, contact tracing and surveillance. <b>Limit onward spread</b>: quarantine, awareness campaigns, rapid-response protocols.</p>"},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["The Director is about to meet the Health Minister and wants a brief update on your findings."],
     listen:["Answer first: adopt the pill vaccine (~11M fewer infections)","Why it matters: A's history of high infection rates","Identify at-risk groups (needs more data)","Border measures for imported cases","Next: affordability and scale, B and C benchmarks, hospitalization data"],
     key:"<p>\"You asked how to prevent an outbreak. We recommend adopting the pill vaccine: slightly less effective than the injection, but its higher coverage could prevent about 11 million infections. That matters because Country A has had much higher infection rates than its neighbors. We should also identify the groups most at risk of hospitalization, which needs more data, and reduce imported cases with entry requirements, border monitoring, targeted testing and quarantine. Next: confirm the pill is affordable and scalable, study Countries B and C, and collect hospitalization rates and condition prevalence by age.\"</p>"}
  ]
},
{
  id:"purefresh", title:"Pure Fresh Sandwiches", type:"Growth strategy", diff:"Easy", mins:30,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["Growth strategy"], sectors:["Retail"], stretch:["Numeracy"], video:true, rating:4.7, downloads:20813},
  plain:"The world's third-largest sandwich chain wants revenue growth to rise from 1% to 3%+ a year. There's no single answer: the candidate collects several opportunities: a coffee price test, sizing premium sandwiches in India, and ways to raise spend per visit, then pulls them together.",
  bottom:"Revenue tree: stores × visits per store × spend per visit. Latte: $2.50 maximizes direct profit ($325 a day), but <b>$2 maximizes revenue</b> and footfall. India premium sandwiches ≈ 1.3B × 40% × 25% × 20% = 26M buyers × 2 a week × $1 ≈ <b>$2.7B a year</b>. Plus in-store levers (layout, bundles, upselling, new products).",
  prompt:["Our client is Pure Fresh Sandwiches, a large sandwich company that competes with Subway and other major chains. It owns and operates the third-largest chain of sandwich stores in the world.","Like its competitors, it sells premium sandwiches, coffees and teas, soft drinks and snacks. Last year it had sales of $5bn.","Growth has slowed in recent years, and a new CEO has been hired to increase profits. A team already works on cost reduction; the client needs help finding revenue opportunities to implement over the next five years.","How can Pure Fresh accelerate revenue growth over the next five years?"],
  clarify:[
    ["What's the growth target?","From 1% a year to 3%+ over this period. The market has also grown 1% a year recently."],
    ["What's in scope?","Opportunities that start paying off within five years. No growth outside the core business, and no new brands."],
    ["Should I look at costs?","No, this is specifically a revenue case."]
  ],
  openListen:["Asks for the target (1% → 3%+)","Notes the market grows only 1%","Confirms scope: revenue, core business, five years"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"2–3 min",
     say:["How would you structure your approach to the problem?"],
     steer:["Original case: if asked, or if the candidate drifts, clarify this is a revenue case, not cost reduction."],
     listen:["Revenue = stores × visits per store × revenue per visit","Stores: existing and new geographies","Visits: more customers, more frequent","Per visit: price, items per visit"],
     check:["More stores: existing geographies","More stores: new geographies","More visits: more customers","More visits: more frequent visits","Per visit: higher average price","Per visit: more items per visit"],
     key:"<p><b>1. More stores</b>: in existing and new geographies. <b>2. More visits per store</b>: more customers, more frequent visits. <b>3. More revenue per visit</b>: higher prices, more items per visit.</p>"},
    {name:"Latte price test", time:"4 min",
     say:["The client wants to compete better on coffee against Starbucks and others. It sells its 'Pure Latte' for $3.00 in the US and has trialed lower prices in some stores. Here are the results. What do you think is the optimal price for the Pure Latte?"],
     give:{when:"Right away (Exhibit 1).",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit 1.</b> Pure Latte price trial</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Price per cup</th><th>$3.00</th><th>$2.50</th><th>$2.00</th></tr><tr><td>Cups sold per store per day</td><td>200</td><td>250</td><td>350</td></tr><tr><td>Cost per cup</td><td>$1.50</td><td>$1.20</td><td>$1.10</td></tr></table></div>`},
     listen:["Profit = (price − cost) × cups","$3: $1.50 × 200 = $300","$2.50: $1.30 × 250 = $325","$2: $0.90 × 350 = $315","Direct profit: $2.50 is best","But the goal is revenue: $2 gives $700 vs. $625 vs. $600, plus more footfall for other items"],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">$3.00: ($3.00 − $1.50) × 200 = $300 profit · $600 revenue\n$2.50: ($2.50 − $1.20) × 250 = $325 profit · $625 revenue\n$2.00: ($2.00 − $1.10) × 350 = $315 profit · $700 revenue</p><p>On direct profit, $2.50 wins. But the client's goal is revenue, and a cheaper latte brings extra footfall and sales of other items, so <b>$2 is the most favorable</b>.</p>",
     stuck:["\"What does the client make on one latte at each price?\""]},
    {name:"India market size", time:"5 min",
     say:["The team is also considering international expansion, in particular launching premium sandwiches in India. Go ahead and estimate the market size for premium sandwiches in India."],
     steer:["Original case: if the candidate is unsure, say we want an annual market size in dollars, and 'premium sandwiches' means only those bought from a chain like the client's, not homemade.","If they go off track, steer them toward urban, middle- and higher-income customers.","Share any of the facts below if prompted."],
     give:{when:"If prompted.", html:"<ul><li>Population of India: <b>1.3 billion</b></li><li>Urban population: <b>40%</b></li><li>Income needed to buy premium sandwiches: <b>top quartile</b> of the urban income distribution</li><li>Expected price: <b>$1</b> per sandwich</li></ul>"},
     num:{min:1.5e9, max:4e9, unit:"$ per year"},
     listen:["Lays out the approach first","1.3B × 40% urban × 25% top quartile ≈ 130M in the market","× 20% who'd buy ≈ 26M customers","× 2 a week × $1 ≈ $52M a week ≈ $2.7B a year","So what: material vs. $5B revenue"],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">1.3B × 40% urban × 25% top quartile ≈ 130M\n× 20% who'd buy (working adults wanting a light lunch) ≈ 26M\n× 2 per week ≈ 52M sandwiches × $1 ≈ $52M a week ≈ $2.7B a year</p><p>Attractive: even a small share would make a material difference to a $5B company.</p>",
     stuck:["\"Who could realistically buy a premium sandwich in India?\""]},
    {name:"Spend per visit", time:"3 min",
     say:["The client wants entirely new ideas too. At the store level, how else could Pure Fresh grow revenue per customer visit, besides changing the price?"],
     steer:["Original case: if the candidate is unsure, clarify that we're focusing on sales per visit, not more visits overall, so ideas like geographic expansion aren't relevant."],
     listen:["Keep people in store longer","Promotions and upselling","Broaden the range"],
     check:["Better layout and design","Better wifi","Charging points and facilities","Bundles","Staff upselling","Visual promotion of higher-value items","New products in existing categories (new coffees)","New categories (mugs, equipment)"],
     key:"<p><b>Keep people in store longer</b>: layout and design, better wifi, charging points. <b>Promotions</b>: bundles, staff upselling, visual promotion of higher-value products. <b>Wider range</b>: new products in existing categories (new coffees) and new categories (mugs, sandwich equipment).</p>"},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["Overall, what would you recommend to the client?"],
     listen:["Restates the goal: grow revenue over 5 years","Three opportunities: pricing (coffee), India (~$2.7B), spend per visit","Next: check whether they reach the 3% target"],
     key:"<p>\"You asked how Pure Fresh can grow revenue over five years. We see at least three major opportunities. First, price optimization: a cheaper coffee draws more customers and lifts sales. Second, geographic expansion, especially India, a potential $2.7B market. Third, raising spend per visit through promotions and a better store environment. Over the coming weeks, we'll test whether these together reach the 3% annual growth target.\"</p>"}
  ]
},
{
  id:"spaceexplorer", title:"Space Explorer", type:"Market entry", diff:"Hard", mins:35,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["Market entry"], sectors:["Leisure & Tourism"], stretch:["Numeracy"], video:true, rating:4.7, downloads:14988},
  plain:"A long-time NASA supplier has built a reusable rocket that could fly 20 tourists around the planet. Should it launch a space tourism business? The candidate sets a ticket price from costs and competitors, finds the breakeven, sizes the wealthy-tourist market step by step, and finds how to beat Virgin Galactic.",
  bottom:"Price between cost per seat ($4M ÷ 20 = $200k) and Virgin ($400k): <b>$300k</b>, so $100k margin → breakeven <b>2,500 tickets</b> ($250M ÷ $100k). Wealthy tourists: $600k holiday ÷ 20% = $3M income → 0.01% × 300M = 30,000 → ⅓ = 10,000 couples → 20,000 US tickets × 2 = <b>~40,000 tickets</b>. Go ahead, and win on the NASA heritage and the vertical launch.",
  prompt:["Our client is a US space exploration company that has supplied NASA for 70 years, taking part in every major space program (e.g. Apollo, Gemini) as a subcontractor providing technologies and rocket parts.","It has developed a technology it believes can send tourists to space for less than current options: a reusable vertical launcher that takes off and lands vertically on its launch pad. It would carry 20 tourists per trip on a 2.5-hour revolution around the planet.","It would be the first time the company builds the whole launcher, sells it and operates it itself. It would be ready for its first commercial flight in 4 years.","Should they go ahead and launch this program?"],
  clarify:[
    ["What's the goal?","Profitability. If the profit potential is attractive, they'll go ahead."],
    ["Is there a profit target or payback period?","No specific profitability target or timeframe to recoup the investment."],
    ["Should we look at alternative investments?","No, they haven't asked us to."]
  ],
  openListen:["Asks the objective (profitability)","Notes: first time building, selling and operating the whole launcher","Notes the 4-year timeline"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"3 min",
     say:["What factors would you consider to answer the client's question?"],
     listen:["Is there a market (can afford, want to)?","Can we win share (competitors, preferences, our offer)?","Can we break even (investment, cost per flight)?","Can we execute (risks, capabilities)?"],
     check:["Market: who can afford it","Market: who wants to do it","Position: competitors","Position: customer preferences","Position: our offering","Breakeven: upfront investment and fixed costs","Breakeven: cost per flight","Risks: accidents, regulation, new competitors","Capabilities: development, sales, operations"],
     probe:["\"What's new for this company compared with its NASA work?\""],
     key:"<p><b>1. Is there a market?</b> People who can afford it, and of those, who want to. <b>2. Can we capture share?</b> Competitors, customer preferences, our offering. <b>3. Can we break even?</b> Upfront investment and fixed costs, cost per flight. <b>4. Can we execute?</b> Risks (accidents, regulation, new entrants) and capabilities (building, selling, operating).</p>"},
    {name:"Price and breakeven", time:"4 min",
     say:["The client agrees a key question is whether there's a market. Before sizing it: what price would you charge for a ticket, and how would you set it?"],
     steer:["Original case: the candidate should realize a price is needed to work out who can afford this. If they don't suggest looking at competitors and our costs, prompt them.","Once they suggest a price, encourage them to use $300,000 for ease of calculation."],
     give:{when:"Once they ask about competitors and costs.",
       html:"<p><b>Competitors</b></p><ul><li><b>Virgin Galactic</b>: ready in 2 years. A shuttle launched from under a plane's belly that lands on a runway; 6 tourists per trip, 2.5-hour revolution around the planet; <b>$400,000</b> per ticket.</li><li><b>Russian Space Agency</b>: <b>$30M</b> per passenger for a 2-week trip to the International Space Station.</li></ul><p><b>Our costs</b></p><ul><li><b>$250M</b> upfront investment</li><li><b>$4M</b> per flight (we can launch as many flights as we want)</li></ul>"},
     num:{value:2500, tol:.02, unit:"tickets to break even at $300k"},
     listen:["Floor: cost per seat = $4M ÷ 20 = $200k","Ceiling: Virgin's comparable offer at $400k (Russia is a different product)","Picks ~$300k","Margin $100k → breakeven $250M ÷ $100k = 2,500 tickets = 125 flights"],
     ifwrong:[["Compares with the $30M Russian trip","\"Is a two-week stay on the space station the same product?\""]],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">Cost per seat = $4M ÷ 20 = $200k (floor) · Virgin = $400k (ceiling) → price $300k\nMargin = $100k per ticket\nBreakeven = $250M ÷ $100k = 2,500 tickets = 125 flights</p>",
     stuck:["\"What does one seat cost us to fly?\""]},
    {name:"Market size", time:"7 min",
     say:["Now, could you size the potential market? You can assume half the global market is in the US. Lay out your approach first."],
     steer:["Original case: if the candidate doesn't lay out an approach, ask for it. Then encourage two steps: (1) how many people can afford this, (2) how many of them want to do it.","Ask what the key types of customers will be: people will make different efforts depending on their motivation. Then ask them to focus on the two segments below, starting with wealthy tourists.","Let them make their own assumptions, but correct them toward the facts below."],
     give:{when:"After they've discussed customer types.",
       html:"<p><b>Two segments</b></p><ul><li><b>Wealthy tourists</b>: spend about the same ($300,000 per person) on their summer holiday; they'll go to space because they can; they travel as a <b>couple</b>.</li><li><b>Passionate</b>: going to space is a lifelong dream; they'll save or borrow for years; they travel <b>alone</b>.</li></ul><p><b>Correct their assumptions toward</b>: US population <b>300M</b>; wealthy tourists spend <b>20%</b> of income on their summer holiday; only <b>0.01%</b> of Americans earn more than <b>$3M</b>; <b>a third</b> of those who can afford it will want to go.</p>"},
     num:{value:40000, tol:.05, unit:"tickets (wealthy tourists, global)"},
     listen:["States the approach: can afford → want to → tickets → global","Couple spends 2 × $300k = $600k on a holiday","$600k ÷ 20% = $3M income → 0.01% × 300M = 30,000 people","⅓ want to → 10,000 → × 2 tickets = 20,000 US tickets","Half the market is US → 40,000 tickets globally","vs. breakeven 2,500: very attractive"],
     ifwrong:[["Counts people, not tickets","\"They travel as couples. How many tickets is that?\""],["Forgets the non-US half","\"What share of the market is in the US?\""]],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">Couple's holiday = 2 × $300k = $600k → income = $600k ÷ 20% = $3M\nPeople earning $3M+ = 0.01% × 300M = 30,000\nWant to go = ⅓ → 10,000 → × 2 tickets = 20,000 US tickets\nUS is half → ~40,000 tickets globally</p><p>Breakeven is only 2,500 tickets, so a small share of this market makes the program profitable.</p>",
     stuck:["\"How much would a couple spend on this trip?\"","\"What income would let them spend that on a holiday?\""]},
    {name:"Passionate segment", time:"2 min",
     say:["What if I tell you the passionate segment is 700 tickets globally?"],
     listen:["Not significant vs. 40,000","Focus product and marketing on wealthy tourists","Still court the passionate as opinion leaders (raffle, promotion)"],
     key:"<p>Small next to 40,000, so <b>focus on wealthy tourists</b> in product and marketing. Still worth engaging the passionate as credible opinion leaders, e.g. a raffle or a promotion for thought leaders.</p>"},
    {name:"Beating Virgin", time:"3 min",
     say:["Virgin Galactic will probably reach the same conclusion and target wealthy tourists too. What can you do that Virgin can't, to beat them in this segment?"],
     listen:["NASA heritage: the 'real astronaut' experience and safety credibility","Vertical launch feels like real spaceflight","Bigger cabin: groups and premium seating","US-based, where half the market is"],
     check:["NASA partnership: training center, launch site, branded suits","Safety credentials from NASA history","Vertical launch = real astronaut experience","Larger cabin: groups","Larger cabin: business/first class at higher prices","US company in the biggest market"],
     key:"<p>Use our <b>70-year NASA history</b>: partner with NASA for a real astronaut experience (training, launch from Cape Canaveral, named space suits) and safety credibility. Our <b>vertical launch</b> feels like real spaceflight, not a plane ride. Our <b>bigger cabin</b> allows groups or premium classes at higher prices. We're <b>US-based</b>, where half the market is.</p>"},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["Overall, what would you recommend to the client?"],
     listen:["Answer first: go ahead","40,000-ticket market vs. 2,500 breakeven","Can beat Virgin: NASA history, vertical launch, bigger cabin","Next: assess and mitigate safety and operational risks (insurance, crew, safeguards)"],
     key:"<p>\"You asked whether to launch this program. It looks very attractive. The wealthy-tourist market is about 40,000 tickets and you break even at 2,500, so a small share recovers the investment. You can beat Virgin: your NASA history, vertical launch and bigger cabin offer a unique, premium 'real astronaut' experience. Over the next few weeks, we'd assess the risks of flying civilians and how to mitigate them: insurance, highly trained crew and other safeguards.\"</p>"}
  ]
},
{
  id:"labdiamonds", title:"Lab-grown Diamonds", type:"Sustainability, Market entry", diff:"Easy", mins:25,
  meta:{kind:"Interviewer-led (classic)", functions:["Sustainability","Market entry"], sectors:["Retail"], stretch:["Creativity"], video:false, rating:null, downloads:null},
  plain:"A premium Southern European jeweler is considering a line of lab-grown diamonds. The candidate structures the decision, calculates the profit after some existing customers switch (cannibalization), reads a survey comparing existing customers with the public, and weighs a separate online sub-brand against adding the line to the main brand.",
  bottom:"Gross profit rises <b>€29.25M → €34.65M (+€5.4M)</b> even with 20% cannibalization. But existing customers see lab-grown as less valuable (brand risk), while the general public shows higher intent, online affinity and gifting potential. Launch as an <b>online-first sub-brand</b>, starting with a pilot.",
  prompt:["Bellagio Jewels is a premium jewelry company operating mainly in Southern Europe. It targets upper-middle-class women aged 30–55, and has built its brand on emotional value, craftsmanship and timeless design.","The CEO is considering launching a new product line of lab-grown diamonds, given growing consumer interest in sustainability and affordability.","Should they do it?"],
  clarify:[
    ["What's the objective?","Decide whether to launch the line, and if so, how."],
    ["Who are the customers?","Upper-middle-class women aged 30–55."],
    ["Where do they sell?","Mainly Southern Europe."]
  ],
  openListen:["Plays back: whether AND how to launch","Notes brand built on emotional value and craftsmanship","Spots the tension: affordable/sustainable vs. premium"],
  steps:[
    {name:"Framework", time:"3 min",
     say:["How would you approach the decision of whether Bellagio should enter the lab-grown diamond segment, and how they should go about it?"],
     listen:["Market demand and competition","Financial impact incl. cannibalization","Brand impact","Launch strategy: segments, brand, channels"],
     check:["Market: size and growth, lab-grown vs. mined","Market: competition","Financial: pricing, margin, cannibalization","Financial: incremental profit","Brand: dilution of 'timeless luxury'?","Brand: can sustainability be premium?","Launch: target segments","Launch: Bellagio brand or sub-brand","Launch: channels (stores, online, new brand)"],
     key:"<p><b>1. Market demand</b>: size and growth of lab-grown vs. mined; competition. <b>2. Financial impact</b>: pricing, margin, cannibalization, incremental profit. <b>3. Brand impact</b>: dilution risk; can sustainability be premium? <b>4. Launch strategy</b>: target segments, own brand or sub-brand, channels.</p>"},
    {name:"Profit impact", time:"4 min",
     say:["Bellagio sells 50,000 diamonds a year at an average of €900, with a 65% gross margin. With a lab-grown line, it estimates 20% of existing customers would switch to lab-grown, priced at €600 with a 75% gross margin. It also forecasts 15,000 new unit sales from first-time buyers. Should it expect annual gross profit to rise or fall?"],
     num:{value:5.4e6, tol:.03, unit:"€ change in gross profit"},
     listen:["Baseline: 50,000 × €900 × 65% = €29.25M","Switched: 10,000 × €600 × 75% = €4.5M","Remaining mined: 40,000 × €900 × 65% = €23.4M","New buyers: 15,000 × €600 × 75% = €6.75M","Total €34.65M → +€5.4M","Sensitivity: higher cannibalization erodes the gain"],
     key:"<p class=\"calc\">Today: 50,000 × €900 × 65% = €29.25M\nSwitchers: 10,000 × €600 × 75% = €4.5M\nRemaining mined: 40,000 × €900 × 65% = €23.4M\nNew buyers: 15,000 × €600 × 75% = €6.75M\nNew total €34.65M → +€5.4M</p><p>Attractive, assuming only 20% cannibalization. Next: test 30–40% cannibalization, and combine with brand survey data to choose integration vs. sub-brand.</p>",
     stuck:["\"Each switching customer pays less. What profit do we make on them now vs. before?\""]},
    {name:"Survey insights", time:"4 min",
     say:["What insights can you draw from this survey about Bellagio's chances of success with lab-grown diamonds?"],
     give:{when:"Right away (Exhibit A).",
       html:`<p><b>Exhibit A.</b> Survey: existing Bellagio customers vs. general public (% agree)</p><div class="tw"><table class="data"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Existing customers</th><th>General public</th></tr><tr><td>I care about sustainability</td><td>82%</td><td>85%</td></tr><tr><td>I would consider buying lab-grown diamonds</td><td>60%</td><td>78%</td></tr><tr><td>I believe lab-grown are less valuable</td><td>58%</td><td>42%</td></tr><tr><td>I would expect to pay less for lab-grown</td><td>92%</td><td>94%</td></tr><tr><td>I associate Bellagio with timeless craftsmanship</td><td>88%</td><td>45%</td></tr><tr><td>I would consider Bellagio for gifting</td><td>52%</td><td>74%</td></tr><tr><td>I would buy jewelry online</td><td>48%</td><td>80%</td></tr></table></div>`},
     listen:["Sustainability matters to both groups: real demand","Existing customers see lab-grown as less valuable: brand risk","Public: higher intent, online and gifting affinity: a new customer base","Opportunity: reach a new, younger, digital-first segment rather than cannibalize"],
     key:"<p>Strong <b>interest in sustainability</b> in both groups. <b>Existing customers</b> see lab-grown as less valuable (58%), a risk for a brand built on craftsmanship. The <b>general public</b> shows higher purchase intent (78%), buys online (80%) and considers Bellagio for gifts (74%). So lab-grown could <b>reach a new, younger, digital-first segment</b> rather than cannibalize the current one.</p>",
     stuck:["\"Where do the two groups differ most?\""]},
    {name:"Sub-brand or main line?", time:"3 min",
     say:["The CMO suggests launching lab-grown as an online-only sub-brand targeting Gen Z for gifting. The new brand would cost €1.5M upfront. What are the pros and cons of a sub-brand vs. integrating lab-grown into the main Bellagio line?"],
     listen:["Sub-brand pros: protects luxury image, fits young online buyers, easier to test, clear sustainability story","Sub-brand cons: €1.5M upfront, complexity","Integration pros: cheaper, faster","Integration cons: brand dilution, confused message, cannibalization","Takes a side: sub-brand, piloted small"],
     key:"<p><b>Sub-brand</b>: protects Bellagio's luxury image, fits younger online buyers, easier to test and scale, clear sustainability positioning; but €1.5M upfront and more complexity. <b>Integration</b>: cheaper, uses existing infrastructure, faster; but risks brand dilution, confuses traditional customers, and cannibalizes the core. On balance, <b>a sub-brand</b>, starting as a limited pilot after customer testing.</p>"},
    {name:"Recommendation", time:"1–2 min",
     say:["Should Bellagio launch a lab-grown diamond line? If so, how?"],
     listen:["Answer first: yes, via an online-first sub-brand","+€5.4M a year even after 20% cannibalization; higher margins","Existing customers see lower value: dilution risk","Non-customers show intent, sustainability focus, gifting","Next: market and competition analysis; pilot with KPIs"],
     key:"<p>\"Bellagio should enter lab-grown diamonds, but through a new online-first sub-brand aimed at younger, sustainability-minded buyers. It adds about €5.4M of gross profit a year even after 20% cannibalization, thanks to higher margins and new customers. Our customers see lab-grown as less valuable, so putting it under the main brand risks dilution, while the public shows higher intent, online buying and gifting potential: a new segment, not overlap. Next: analyze market demand and competition, then pilot a limited online collection to track acquisition, brand perception and cannibalization.\"</p>"}
  ]
}
);
