Object.assign(window.CASE_SCRIPTS,{
viraloutbreak:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me make sure I understand.</b> A new airborne virus could cause an epidemic, and Country A, which has suffered badly before, <b>wants to prevent an outbreak</b>. Could I ask how an outbreak is defined, how big the population is, and whether a vaccine exists?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd follow the path of a single infection.</b> <b>Prevention</b>: do people know how to protect themselves, is there an affordable, accessible vaccine, are there masks, and are measures like quarantine ready? <b>Diagnosis</b>: is there a reliable, accessible test, with enough capacity and fast results? <b>Treatment</b>: is there an effective treatment, and enough capacity to treat patients? <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Flu season data":"<p><b>Exhibit 1</b>: Country A's infections rise much faster than its neighbors' and it's the first past the outbreak threshold, so <b>its prevention looks weaker</b>; B and C hold infections down and could be benchmarks. <b>Exhibit 2 needs care</b>: older hospitalized patients more often have pre-existing conditions, from 19% to 76%. <b>But it doesn't prove older people are more at risk</b>: we'd need hospitalization rates by age and how common these conditions are overall.</p>",
  "Pill vs. injection":"<p><b>I'll compare expected infection rates.</b> <b>Injection</b>: 60% at 0.01% plus 40% unvaccinated at 10%, so about <b>4.0%</b>. <b>Pill</b>: 90% at 0.83% plus 10% at 10%, about <b>1.75%</b>. That's 2.26 points lower, and across 500 million people, <b>about 11 million fewer infections</b>. <b>So adopt the pill</b>: coverage matters more than per-person effectiveness. Next, check cost and distribution at scale.</p>",
  "Imported cases":"<p><b>Three layers of defense.</b> <b>Keep infected people out</b>: vaccination or test requirements, restrictions on high-risk regions, health declarations. <b>Catch cases early</b>: monitoring at airports and borders, testing high-risk travelers, contact tracing. <b>Stop onward spread</b>: quarantine, public awareness, and a rapid-response plan.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>We recommend adopting the pill vaccine</b>: less effective per person, but its reach could prevent about 11 million infections, which matters given Country A's history of higher infection rates. We also need more data to identify who's most at risk of hospitalization, and border measures against imported cases. <b>Next</b>: confirm cost and scale, learn from Countries B and C, and collect hospitalization data by age.</p>"
 }},
purefresh:{
 open:"<p><b>To recap</b>: Pure Fresh is the world's third-largest sandwich chain, with $5 billion in sales, but growth has slowed. <b>We're looking for revenue opportunities over the next five years</b>, while another team handles costs. Could I ask what growth target we're aiming for, how fast the market grows, and whether new brands or businesses are in scope?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>Revenue is stores times visits per store times spend per visit, so I'd grow each.</b> <b>More stores</b>, in current and new countries. <b>More visits</b>, by attracting new customers and bringing existing ones back more often. <b>More spend per visit</b>, through pricing and selling more items per visit. <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Latte price test":"<p><b>Profit per store per day is margin times cups.</b> At $3, $1.50 times 200 is <b>$300</b>. At $2.50, $1.30 times 250 is <b>$325</b>. At $2, 90 cents times 350 is <b>$315</b>. On direct profit, $2.50 wins. <b>But our goal is revenue</b>: $2 brings in $700 a day versus $625, and more people coming in for coffee also buy sandwiches. <b>So I'd go with $2.</b></p>",
  "India market size":"<p><b>I'll go from population to buyers to sales.</b> 1.3 billion people, 40% urban, top quarter of urban incomes: <b>about 130 million</b> who could afford it. Say 20% actually want a premium sandwich, mostly working adults at lunch: <b>26 million</b>. Two a week each is 52 million sandwiches at $1, <b>$52 million a week, or about $2.7 billion a year</b>. <b>So what</b>: for a $5 billion company, even a small share matters.</p>",
  "Spend per visit":"<p><b>Three ideas.</b> <b>Keep people longer</b>: a nicer layout, good wifi, charging points. <b>Sell more</b>: meal bundles, staff suggesting add-ons, and displays for higher-value items. <b>Sell new things</b>: new coffees in the current range, and new categories like branded mugs.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>You asked how to grow revenue over five years. We see three opportunities.</b> <b>Pricing</b>: a cheaper coffee draws in customers and lifts total sales. <b>India</b>: a $2.7 billion market for premium sandwiches. <b>Spend per visit</b>: promotions and a better store experience. <b>Next</b>, we'll size each one to check that together they reach 3% growth.</p>"
 }},
spaceexplorer:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me play that back.</b> A long-time NASA supplier has a reusable rocket that could take 20 tourists around the planet, ready in four years. <b>It would build, sell and operate it for the first time. Should it go ahead?</b> Could I ask what the goal is, whether there's a profit target or payback period, and whether we're comparing with other investments?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd ask four questions.</b> <b>Is there a market?</b> Who can afford it, and who wants to go. <b>Can we win share?</b> Competitors, what customers value, and our offer. <b>Can we break even?</b> The upfront investment against the margin per flight. <b>Can we execute?</b> Safety and regulatory risks, and whether we can sell and operate, which is new for us. <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Price and breakeven":"<p><b>I'd price between our cost and the closest competitor.</b> Could I ask what competitors charge and what our costs are? <i>[Virgin $400k; $4M per flight.]</i> A flight costs $4 million for 20 seats, so <b>$200,000 per seat</b> is our floor. Virgin's similar trip at $400,000 is the ceiling; Russia's $30 million station trip is a different product. <b>So about $300,000</b>, giving $100,000 margin per ticket. <b>Breakeven: $250 million divided by $100,000, or 2,500 tickets</b>, about 125 flights.</p>",
  "Market size":"<p><b>Two steps: who can afford it, then who wants to.</b> A wealthy couple would spend $600,000. If a summer holiday is 20% of income, that's <b>$3 million a year</b>, which only 0.01% of Americans earn: <b>30,000 people</b>. A third want to go, so <b>10,000</b>, each buying two tickets: <b>20,000 US tickets</b>. The US is half the market, so <b>about 40,000 tickets worldwide</b>. <b>Against a 2,500-ticket breakeven, that's very attractive.</b></p>",
  "Passionate segment":"<p><b>700 tickets is small next to 40,000, so wealthy tourists should be our focus</b> in product and marketing. But the passionate are great ambassadors, so I'd still reach them, for example with a raffle or seats for well-known space enthusiasts.</p>",
  "Beating Virgin":"<p><b>We can offer the real astronaut experience.</b> <b>Our NASA history</b>: partner with NASA for training, a launch from Cape Canaveral and named space suits, and borrow its safety credibility. <b>Vertical launch</b> feels like real spaceflight, not a plane ride. <b>Our bigger cabin</b> fits groups or premium classes at higher prices. And <b>we're American</b>, where half the market is.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>Go ahead.</b> The wealthy-tourist market is about 40,000 tickets and you break even at 2,500, so you only need a small share. You can beat Virgin with your NASA heritage, vertical launch and premium cabin. <b>Next</b>: we'll assess the risks of flying civilians and how to manage them, from insurance to crew training and other safeguards.</p>"
 }},
labdiamonds:{
 open:"<p><b>To recap</b>: Bellagio is a premium jeweler built on craftsmanship and timeless design, considering a lab-grown diamond line for sustainability- and price-minded buyers. <b>The question is whether to launch it, and how.</b> Could I ask who its customers are, where it sells, and how its sales are split between stores and online?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd answer 'whether' and 'how' in four parts.</b> <b>Demand</b>: how big and fast-growing lab-grown is, and how competitive. <b>Financials</b>: price, margin and how much we'd cannibalize. <b>Brand</b>: would it dilute our timeless luxury image, or can sustainability be premium? <b>Launch</b>: which customers, under the Bellagio name or a sub-brand, and through which channels. <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Profit impact":"<p><b>I'll compare gross profit before and after.</b> Today: 50,000 at €900 at 65%, <b>€29.25 million</b>. After: the 10,000 who switch earn €4.5 million, the 40,000 who stay €23.4 million, and 15,000 new buyers €6.75 million, so <b>€34.65 million</b>. <b>That's €5.4 million more a year.</b> It depends on cannibalization staying near 20%, so I'd test 30 to 40%.</p>",
  "Survey insights":"<p><b>Two different audiences.</b> Both care about sustainability. But <b>58% of our customers think lab-grown is less valuable</b>, a risk for a brand built on craftsmanship. <b>The public is more open</b>: 78% would consider it, 80% buy jewelry online, and 74% would consider us for gifts. <b>So lab-grown is a chance to win new, younger, online customers</b>, not just shift our own.</p>",
  "Sub-brand or main line?":"<p><b>A sub-brand protects the core but costs more.</b> Pros: it shields Bellagio's luxury image, suits young online buyers, and is easy to test. Cons: €1.5 million upfront and more complexity. <b>Integration</b> is cheaper and faster, but risks diluting the brand, confusing loyal customers and cannibalizing more. <b>On balance I'd go with a sub-brand</b>, starting with a small pilot.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>Yes, launch lab-grown diamonds, but as an online-first sub-brand.</b> It adds about €5.4 million of gross profit a year, even after some customers switch. Our customers see lab-grown as less valuable, so the main brand is risky, but younger online buyers are keen. <b>Next</b>: size the market and competitors, and pilot a small online collection to track new customers, brand perception and cannibalization.</p>"
 }}
});
