Object.assign(window.CASE_SCRIPTS,{
redwhisk:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me make sure I understand.</b> BigCart's new venture fund is considering its first investment, in Red Whisk, a meal-kit subscription company. <b>It should invest only if Red Whisk is a profitable investment AND advances BigCart's 'every meal occasion' strategy.</b> Could I ask how Red Whisk compares with competitors, and whether we need to value the deal?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>BigCart should invest if three things are true.</b> <b>One, the meal-kit market is attractive</b>: big, growing, and not too competitive. <b>Two, Red Whisk has a path to profit</b>: healthy volume and price, and costs (ingredients, delivery, marketing) that fall as it grows. <b>Three, there are real synergies</b>: cheaper sourcing, delivery and marketing for Red Whisk, and a new offer for BigCart's customers. <b>I'd start with the market.</b></p>",
  "Market and competition":"<p><b>The market is attractive but crowded.</b> It grew about four and a half times in four years, to $7 billion; growth is slowing, which is normal as it matures. And if meal kits take share from grocers, BigCart should be there. <b>But</b> competitors went from four to six, <b>Red Whisk's share fell from 50% to 30%</b>, and rankings change every year. So barriers are low, rivals may be VC-funded, and customers aren't loyal: expect price wars.</p>",
  "Path to profitability":"<p><b>Red Whisk loses more money each year</b>, so I'd check each line. Gross margin is improving, from 20% to 30%, and overheads are flat. <b>The problem is marketing</b>: from 20% to 35% of sales. Could I check acquisition cost against customer value? <b>CAC</b>: $735 million over 1.43 million new customers, about <b>$515</b>. <b>LTV</b>: from the churn table, customers stay about 7.5 months, ordering 3.5 boxes at $5 margin, about <b>$131</b>. <b>So it pays four dollars to earn one, when healthy is one to two.</b> Churn also worsens with each cohort, likely because free-box customers don't stick.</p>",
  "Synergies":"<p><b>I'd split synergies into cost and revenue, and check feasibility.</b> <b>Cost</b>: BigCart's purchasing terms, its delivery fleet, and its loyalty program for cheaper marketing. <b>Revenue</b>: selling kits in BigCart's stores and website. <b>But</b> Red Whisk ships everything on Tuesdays, which BigCart's fleet may not handle, and BigCart's own in-store kits failed. <b>With 5% off COGS and 20% off marketing</b>, we save about $75 million plus $150 million, $225 million. <b>Red Whisk still loses about $280 million.</b></p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>Don't invest in Red Whisk now, for three reasons.</b> The market is growing but crowded, and Red Whisk keeps losing share. It loses more every year, spending about $515 to win a customer worth about $131. And even $225 million of synergies leaves it deep in the red. <b>But meal kits matter strategically</b>, so revisit in one to two years: if losses fall to about $225 million, BigCart could invest and use the synergies to reach profitability.</p>"
 }},
taxiempire:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me play that back.</b> Your friend earns 10% after tax on €50,000 and is considering starting a taxi company in her town, managing it while hired drivers drive. <b>The question is whether she'd beat 10%.</b> Could I ask whether she cares about anything beyond return, like risk or growing the business?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>Three questions.</b> <b>Does one taxi beat 10%?</b> That's the upfront cost against the profit after the taxi's running costs and overhead. <b>Can she actually run it?</b> Licenses, finding drivers, and getting customers. <b>Can it scale safely?</b> Demand for more taxis, managing a bigger team, and long-term risks like costs, regulation and competition. <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Revenue estimate":"<p><b>I'll start from capacity.</b> 8 hours a day for 250 days at 50 km an hour is <b>100,000 km a year</b>. If half of that has a paying passenger at €1.40 a km, that's <b>€70,000 of revenue</b>. To compare with her 10%, I now need costs.</p>",
  "Return on equity":"<p><b>I'll build a quick P&L.</b> Running costs: 100,000 km at 20 cents, €20,000, leaving €50,000. Minus €30,000 overhead: <b>€20,000 EBITDA</b>. The €50,000 car over five years is €10,000 of depreciation, so €10,000 before tax, and after 30% tax, <b>€7,000 net</b>. That's a 10% net margin and a <b>14% return on equity</b>, <b>beating her 10%</b>.</p>",
  "Second taxi with a loan":"<p><b>Two taxis double everything</b>: €20,000 of operating profit. The €50,000 loan at 8% costs €4,000, so €16,000 before tax and <b>€11,200 after tax</b>. Her equity is still €50,000, so <b>return on equity rises to 22.4%</b>, even though the margin falls to 8%. <b>That's leverage.</b> I'd check whether there's demand for a second taxi and how much more of her time it takes.</p>",
  "Risks":"<p><b>Four risks, in order of importance.</b> <b>Forecast error</b>: everything hinges on 50% utilization; if it's 40%, the profit mostly disappears. <b>Leverage</b>: the loan adds fixed costs, so a bad year hurts more. <b>The market</b>: new competitors, rising fuel and wages, regulation, and substitutes like scooters. <b>Her time</b>: all this to go from about €5,000 to €11,000 a year.</p>",
  "Diversifying services":"<p><b>Three ideas.</b> <b>Pooled rides</b>, like school runs and airport transfers. <b>Urgent deliveries</b>, such as medicine for hospitals or spare parts for businesses. And <b>night services</b>, when there's less competition.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I wouldn't start the taxi business yet.</b> On paper it beats her 10%: 14% with one taxi, about 22% with a second on a loan. <b>But</b> it only lifts her income from about €5,000 to €11,000, while risking her capital and taking her time. <b>Next</b>, I'd look at raising returns, like two drivers on longer shifts or premium services, and validate utilization before she commits.</p>"
 }},
childplacement:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me make sure I understand.</b> Germany's 400 districts care for 105,000 children, 55% in costly residential care and 45% in foster homes, and costs have risen 15% in five years. <b>We need to find ways to cut costs, without harming children's welfare.</b> Could I ask who decides on placements, whether there's a savings target, and what's driven the 15% rise?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>A district's cost is roughly the number of children, times how severe their needs are, times the cost of care.</b> <b>Number of children</b>: driven by deprivation, asylum seekers, and things like substance abuse. <b>Severity</b>: older children and abuse cases need more, and early intervention can prevent severe cases. <b>Cost of care</b>: whether districts provide care when needed, the mix of residential and foster care, and the price per night in each area. <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Spend vs. children in care":"<p><b>The number of children in care explains about 44% of the difference in spend.</b> Each child costs about €100,000 a year, which is very high. <b>Prosperous districts</b> have fewer children in care and spend less per child, probably because cases are less severe and foster families easier to find. <b>Deprived districts are all over the place</b>, which suggests different local policies, and a risk that some children who need care aren't getting it.</p>",
  "What else drives cost?":"<p><b>Spend is the number of children, times the cost of each type of care, times the mix.</b> Residential care costs about <b>five times</b> foster care, €4,000 versus €800 a week. <b>But the price of each type barely explains spend</b>: R² is close to zero. Since the number of children explains about half, <b>the mix between residential and foster care must be the other big driver.</b></p>",
  "More foster places":"<p><b>Three ways.</b> <b>Recruit more foster families</b>: advertise through churches and community groups, target people with teaching or care backgrounds or spare rooms, pay more, and make it easier with training, 24/7 support and holiday cover. <b>Ask existing families to take another child.</b> <b>Free up places</b> by returning children home as soon as it's safe.</p>",
  "Shifting the mix":"<p><b>An average district spends about €24.6 million</b>: 140 children in residential care at €150,000, and 120 in foster care at €30,000. <b>10% is €2.46 million</b>. Better foster support adds 10% for the 120 foster children, €0.36 million, so <b>we need to save €2.8 million</b>. Each child moved saves €150,000 minus €33,000, <b>about €120,000</b>. That's about <b>23 children, or 16% of residential care</b>. <b>So what: very achievable</b>, given countries like the UK.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>Costs are driven by how many children are in care and how many are in residential care, which costs five times more.</b> The biggest lever is <b>more and better-supported foster care</b>: moving just 16% of residential children into foster homes cuts total cost by 10%. We'd also <b>share best practices</b> between districts on when and how to provide care; that may not save money, but it makes sure every child who needs care gets it. <b>Next</b>: a plan for each district.</p>"
 }}
});
